{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '1,105p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    56\tthe stream's own.\n    57\t\n    58\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    59\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    60\t\n    61\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    62\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Host Boundaries\n    65\t\n    66\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    67\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    68\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    71\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    72\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    73\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    74\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    75\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    76\t\n    77\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    78\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    79\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    80\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    81\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    84\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    85\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    86\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    87\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    88\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    89\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    90\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    91\tnot an upward callback.\n    92\t\n    93\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    94\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    95\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    96\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n    97\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n    98\tfor facts it owns.\n    99\t\n   100\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   101\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   102\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   103\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   104\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   105\t\n   106\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   107\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n   108\thappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n   109\tevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n   110\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   111\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   112\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   113\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   114\t\n   115\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   116\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   117\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   118\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   119\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   120\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   121\t\n   122\t## Architectural Commitments\n   123\t\n   124\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n   125\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n   126\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n   127\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n   128\t\n   129\t## Design Principles\n   130\t\n   131\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n   132\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n   133\t\n   134\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   135\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   136\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   137\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   138\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   139\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   140\tretired `:global` tag in\n   141\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   142\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   143\t\n   144\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   145\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   146\t\n   147\t## Code Style\n   148\t\n   149\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   150\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   151\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   152\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   153\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   154\t\n   155\t## Development Workflow\n   156\t\n   157\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   158\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n   159\t- Do not optimize prematurely.\n   160\t- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n   161\t- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n   162\t\n   163\t### Problem Decomposition\n   164\t\n   165\tIf a problem appears complex:\n   166\t1. Decompose it into streams.\n   167\t2. Identify invariants.\n   168\t3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n   169\t4. Make causality explicit.\n   170\t5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n   171\t\n   172\t## Operational & Specialized Guides\n   173\t\n   174\t- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n   175\t- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n   176\t- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n   177\t- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n   178\t- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n     1\t# DaoStream Design\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does\n     4\tnot redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only\n     5\tafter this contract is implemented and tested.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Purpose\n     8\t\n     9\tThis contract derives from the foundational axioms and non-negotiable\n    10\tinvariants of [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Two axioms bear on it\n    11\tdirectly, named here once so the sections below can cite rather than restate\n    12\tthem:\n    13\t\n    14\t1. **Everything is a Stream** — all IO and data flow through append-only\n    15\t   streams.\n    16\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics** — data is syntax; semantics emerge\n    17\t   only through interpretation. One truth, many perspectives.\n    18\t\n    19\tDaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world — passive because\n    20\tinterpretation and execution must not collapse into one layer. Axiom 1 is\n    21\twhat makes this the layer every host boundary crosses. A stream carries\n    22\tvalues and decides nothing about them:\n    23\t\n    24\t- **What the values mean** is not its business — Axiom 2. An element\n    25\t  judged, filtered, or typed at this layer destroys the perspectives nobody\n    26\t  has taken yet.\n    27\t- **Which interpreter observes them** is not recorded — a registry of who\n    28\t  is listening would be hidden global state.\n    29\t- **When an interpreter runs** is not its decision — \"do not introduce\n    30\t  callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream\", seen from the stream's\n    31\t  side: DaoStream invokes nothing, so control is never inverted and\n    32\t  causality stays explicit (see The IO Model).\n    33\t\n    34\tThe Invariants below are these denials in operational form.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe design separates five concepts:\n    37\t\n    38\t1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.\n    39\t2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the\n    40\t   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.\n    41\t3. A **logical-stream identity** is transport-independent plain data shared by\n    42\t   every handle and descriptor for one stream.\n    43\t4. A **portable descriptor** is reachability data used to attach to an existing\n    44\t   stream; it includes the logical-stream identity but is not that identity.\n    45\t5. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one\n    46\t   logical stream.\n    47\t\n    48\tCreation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification or an\n    49\tidentity. A handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned\n    50\tstate.\n    51\t\n    52\tSeven operations range over them, each returning an outcome map (see\n    53\tResult Convention):\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |\n    60\t| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |\n    61\t| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |\n    62\t| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |\n    63\t| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |\n    64\t\n    65\tA handle implements the subset of the **handle surface** its transport\n    66\tdeclares (see Surfaces). `create!` and `attach!` are not part of it: they\n    67\tconsume no handle and produce one.\n    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n    88\t\n    89\tEvery clause below must be consistent with these invariants, and most derive\n    90\tfrom one directly. Where a clause is instead an interoperability decision — the\n    91\tdescriptor's properties, the result keyword namespace, the anchor set — it is\n    92\tstated as a decision rather than dressed as a deduction. A proposed addition\n    93\tthat contradicts an invariant does not belong in this contract; one that merely\n    94\tcannot be deduced from them must say which decision it is and why.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
