o it\\n   105\\t  can become the deferred effect-stream write path in `dao.jing.md` without\\n   106\\t  a contract break; the synchronous function handle is its present-day\\n   107\\t  shape, not a commitment. The put half is effect-shaped as stated; the get\\n   108\\t  half becomes effect-shaped once its outcome is the explicit\\n   109\\t  `{:found? boolean, :bytes b}` envelope — `dao.jing.remote` already\\n   110\\t  answers the `{:found? boolean, :value v}` shape this generalizes\\n   111\\t  (`remote.cljc:55-60,84-85`; `:bytes` is this plan's future key, not\\n   112\\t  `:value`, since the value here is a byte representation, not a decoded\\n   113\\t  Clojure value) — with bytes in a portable byte representation — the\\n   114\\t  caller-supplied not-found sentinel is a wrapper-level convenience over\\n   115\\t  that envelope, not part of the contract's streamable shape.\\n   116\\t- Encode once before a write, derive the address from those bytes, and pass\\n   117\\t  those same bytes to the backend. On `:present`, read back and verify the\\n   118\\t  bytes by hash and byte-for-byte equality with the proposed payload. Never\\n   119\\t  overwrite conflicting content. Return an address only after\\n   120\\t  the backend reports its applicable storage guarantee.\\n   121\\t- The wrapper derives and verifies addresses; a backend re-verifies a put\\n   122\\t  exactly when bytes arrive from an untrusted party, such as a remote/DHT\\n   123\\t  server checking a client's claim.\\n   124\\t- Verify every retrieved payload against its requested address before\\n   125\\t  decoding exactly one supported value. Canonicality verification (decode,\\n   126\\t  re-encode, byte-compare) occurs exactly once at each ingress of bytes Jing\\n   127\\t  did not encode itself: remote/DHT receipt and file replay acceptance,\\n   128\\t  before storage, caching, or exposure. Ordinary reads of accepted snapshots\\n   129\\t  hash-verify and decode without re-encoding. Validation belongs to the\\n   130\\t  shared codec/wrapper at those boundaries, not to backend value handlers;\\n   131\\t  immutable local forwarding does not repeat ingress validation. Missing\\n   132\\t  content remains distinct from stored nil, which has a CBOR representation.\\n   133\\t- Store immutable snapshots. Memory backends copy byte arrays on insertion\\n   134\\t  and retrieval so caller mutation cannot change previously addressed\\n   135\\t  content. Other backends must provide the same observable isolation.\\n   136\\t- Keep backend handles and composition explicit. Introduce no global\\n   137\\t  backend registry, ambient codec options, or domain-specific interpretation.\\n   138\\t\\n   139\\t## Encoding contract\\n   140\\t\\n   367\\thead) over decimal `1.00` (exponent `-2`, mantissa `100`, a two-byte\\n   368\\tshortest head); (3) if the two encodings are the same length too, return\\n   369\\twhichever sorts first by canonical CBOR bytes, the same unsigned bytewise\\n   370\\torder this profile already uses for map keys and set elements, so no new\\n   371\\tordering machinery is needed for the final, rarely-reached tiebreak. All\\n   372\\tthree cases are independent of argument order and of which host runs them:\\n   373\\t`(min 1 1.0)` and `(min 1.0 1)` both return `1`.\\n   374\\t\\n   375\\t- Provide a portable `float64` constructor/carrier. JavaScript callers use\\n   376\\t  it for integral floating-point values such as `1.0`; ordinary integral\\n   377\\t  JavaScript numbers remain integers, except native negative zero, which\\n   378\\t  is floating-point content so its sign survives. Decoding floating-point content on\\n   379\\t  JavaScript returns the carrier so re-encoding cannot lose its kind.\\n   380\\t- Encode floating-point content on every host as tag 27,\\n   381\\t  `dao.jing/float64`, with eight big-endian IEEE-754 bytes. This avoids\\n   382\\t  Boring's native-number differences between JVM and JavaScript, verified\\n   383\\t  against its compatibility policy above: integral JS numbers encode as\\n   384\\t  integers while JVM `1.0` remains a float under `:canonical`.\\n   385\\t  Widen native float32 values to float64, preserve signed zero and\\n   386\\t  infinities, and canonicalize NaNs to quiet NaN bits `0x7ff8000000000000`.\\n   387\\t  **The Jing profile emits no native CBOR floating-point whatsoever**;\\n   388\\t  Boring's canonical float narrowing and NaN encoding never govern Jing\\n   389\\t  floating-point bytes. The carrier is Jing-owned; upstream has no float64\\n   390\\t  wrapper (verified against Boring `data.cljc`, 2026-09-16).\\n   391\\t- Use CBOR integers and bignum tags 2/3 for exact integers. Never convert\\n   392\\t  large integers through a floating-point intermediate. Reject unsafe\\n   393\\t  integral JavaScript Number inputs; callers must supply BigInt for exact\\n   631\\t  arithmetic builtins over carriers\\n   632\\t  reject loudly; and `[e a 1 t m]` versus `[e a 1.0 t m]` collapse to one\\n   633\\t  covered-index entry while keeping two distinct content addresses and not\\n   634\\t  unifying against each other in a query.\\n   635\\t- Malformed and noncanonical encodings; duplicate keys; unknown tags;\\n   636\\t  duplicate set elements and decoded equality collapse; TaggedValue and\\n   637\\t  unsupported SimpleValue; native CBOR floats; invalid UTF-8; trailing data;\\n   638\\t  unsupported host values; address mismatches. Hash-valid noncanonical\\n   639\\t  payloads fail at ingress; repeated local hydration hash-verifies every\\n   640\\t  time without repeating canonical re-encoding.\\n   641\\t- File reopen, duplicate insertion, durability failures, torn tails,\\n   642\\t  complete corrupt frames, raw digest length/mismatch, and nondestructive\\n   643\\t  rejection of old EDN stores (including those with torn tails) and\\n   644\\t  unrecognizable first frames.\\n   645\\t- Memory/file/remote/DHT agreement on addresses and decoded content, including\\n   646\\t  metadata, binary values, rich numbers, stored nil, and missing content.\\n   647\\t- Network corruption, malformed Base64, mixed-version peers in both\\n   648\\t  directions (including Base64-shaped legacy strings), and peer failure.\\n   649\\t  DHT final-envelope sizes at and beyond 1200 bytes for puts and replies,\\n   650\\t  local-only oversized writes, and a WebSocket round trip beyond that\\n   651\\t  budget with no new Jing cap.\\n   652\\t- Existing Jing, published-index, AST-addressing, and continuation tests.\\n   653\\t- An independent CBOR reader inspecting fixtures, with explicit handlers for\\n   654\\t  Jing's named extensions and the correct deterministic ordering rule.\\n  1494\\t              (when (or (nil? e) (nil? m))\\n  1495\\t                (refuse :malformed-number\\n  1496\\t                        \\\"decimal component is not an integer\\\"))\\n  1497\\t              (decimal (b->int e) m))\\n  1498\\t          30 (let [[n d] (or (pair-items p)\\n  1499\\t                             (refuse\\n  1500\\t                               :malformed-number\\n  1501\\t                               \\\"rational payload is not [numerator denominator]\\\"))\\n  1502\\t                   n (int-item bs n true)\\n  1503\\t                   d (int-item bs d true)]\\n  1504\\t               (when (or (nil? n) (nil? d) (not (pos? (bsign d))))\\n  1505\\t                 (refuse :malformed-number\\n   253\\t                                   false)\\n   254\\t            :else (recur (inc i))))))))\\n   255\\t\\n   256\\t\\n   257\\t(defn- ident-parts-ok?\\n   258\\t  \\\"An identifier classifies only when its namespace and name are both\\n   259\\t   well-formed UTF-16: ident parts are strings under NFC.\\\"\\n   260\\t  [x]\\n   261\\t  (and (or (nil? (namespace x))\\n   262\\t           (well-formed-utf16? (namespace x)))\\n   263\\t       (well-formed-utf16? (name x))))\\n   264\\t\\n   265\\t\\n   266\\t(defn canonica