1 712 713 714 715 716 717 718\\n+      719 720 721 722 723 724 725 726 727 728 729 731 734 735 736 738 739\\n+      740 741 742 743 744 745 746 747 748 749 750 752 753 754 757 758 760\\n+      761 762 763 765 766 767 768 769 771 774 775 776 777 779 780 781 782\\n+      783 784 785 786 789 790 792 794 796 797 798 799 800 801 803 804 805\\n+      806 807 808 809 810 812 813 814 815 816 817 818 819 820 821 822 823\\n+      824 825 827 828 829 831 832 834 835 836 837 839 840 842 843 844 845\\n+      846 848 850 851 852 853 854 856 858 860 861 863 864 867 868 869 870\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790264589986-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:88:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_44\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,190p' collab/1790245519256-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md; rg -n 'order-normalize|order-normalized-print|canonical-print|put-content-fn|get-content-fn' src test docs/design/dao.jing.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md | head -65; git diff --unified=0 | perl -ne 'if (/\\\"'^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+\\\\\\\\+\\\\\\\\+ b\\\\\\\\/(.*)/) {\\\"'$f=$1; $ln=0} elsif (/''^@@.*'\\\"\\\\\\\\+(\\\\\\\\d+)(?:,(\\\\\\\\d+))?/) {\\\"'$ln=$1-1} elsif (/''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\+(?\\\"'!'\\\"\\\\\\\\+)(.*)/) {\\\"'$ln++; $s=$1; $a=($s =~ /[''^'\\\"\\\\\\\\x00-\\\\\\\\x7f]/); \\\"'$l=length($s); if($a || $l>80){print \\\"$f:$ln \\\",($a?\\\"nonASCII \\\":\\\"\\\"),($l>80?\\\"len=$l\\\":\\\"\\\"),\\\"'\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"}} elsif (/\\\"'^ /) {$ln++}'\\\"' | head -45\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"I’m using the `code-review-pr` skill because this is a branch-level adversarial review. I’ll first load its review protocol and the four governing design documents, then inspect the branch diff and run only read-only checks/tests that do not modify repository state.\\nThe governing contract is clear on the key failure modes: one CBOR encoder, strict ingress canonicality, byte-snapshot immutability, and nondestructive file replay before torn-tail truncation. I’m now establishing the exact branch delta and will trace those requirements through implementation and tests rather than relying on the task’s file list alone.\\nThe core swap is indeed direct (`canonical-bytes` delegates only to `cbor/encode`), and memory/file now retain encoded byte snapshots. I’m checking the less obvious edges next: replay’s algorithm reconstruction, canonical-frame rejection before truncation, mutation isolation on returned binary values, and whether the updated tests actually exercise these paths.\\nI found a concrete immutability breach: the memory handle now exposes its atom containing mutable host byte arrays, so callers can mutate stored addressed content behind the API. I’m doing a final static verification pass now, including the explicit ASCII/80-column gate and checking whether the tests cover the new byte helpers and replay format.\\nThe focused JVM suite passes (80 tests, 3,200 assertions), and the changed implementation/test sources pass clj-kondo and cljstyle. Those checks do not cover the exposed-byte mutation path or enforce the requested ASCII/80-column limits; both remain review findings. I’m consolidating the final evidence now, including replay’s exact algorithm-selection behavior.\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-24 10:33:59 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 17:33:59 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\nVerdict: request changes.\\n\\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:104) | The public handle exposes `:state`, whose content now contains mutable host byte arrays. A reproduction mutated stored `[1 2]` into `[1 3]`; `mem/entries` returned the forged value while `jing/get` detected an integrity error. `entries` also decodes without address verification at line 118. | Keep the atom inaccessible, expose a closure-based snapshot view, and use `segment-value` for verified decoding. Add mutation-isolation tests for both input and exposed state/output.\\n\\nP1 | [src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:457) | The required shared byte-store boundary was not implemented. `segment-key` encodes once, then `materialize!` passes the original value to the backend; memory and file encode it again at [mem.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:38) and [file.cljc:344](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:344). Instrumentation confirmed two encoder calls for one insertion, contrary to the encode-once contract at [dao.jing.cbor.md:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:98). | Encode once in the Jing wrapper, derive the address from those bytes, and delegate through `:put-bytes-fn`/`:get-bytes-fn`; keep public APIs value-facing.\\n\\nP2 | [docs/design/dao.jing.md:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:190) | The authoritative design still declares the order-normalized printer current, lists it as an open item at line 486, and describes file records as `[address payload]` at line 407. This contradicts the implemented clean break. | Replace the transitional-printer sections with the canonical-CBOR and byte-store contracts, document `[digest payload-bytes]`, and retire the superseded open items.\\n\\nP2 | [test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:222) | The file fail-closed tests still inject EDN text at lines 229–235. They prove that arbitrary non-CBOR fails, but do not exercise valid-CBOR wrong shapes, bad digest lengths, digest mismatches, noncanonical payload bytes, or mutation isolation. | Construct raw CBOR frames for every replay refusal class and add binary/metadata/rich-number and byte-mutation round trips.\\n\\nP3 | [test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/data/psset_fixtures.cljc:1) | The strict hygiene gate fails: this generated file contains non-ASCII and 860 lines over 80 columns; representative long output starts at line 12. Other named files also contain non-ASCII or long lines, including [hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc:94) and [digest-table.edn:104](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:104). | Update the fixture generator to emit ASCII and wrap address literals, regenerate, and reflow the remaining named files.\\n\\nConfirmed correct:\\n\\n- `canonical-bytes` delegates directly to `cbor/encode`; the three printer functions are absent from production sources.\\n- `segment-matches?` is total, while `segment-bytes` and `segment-value` perform the expected hash/codec validation.\\n- File frames are canonical CBOR byte-string pairs. Replay infers the algorithm by testing the digest against each registered algorithm, reconstructs the address from the first match, rejects no-match frames, validates all complete frames, and only then truncates a torn tail: [file.cljc:116](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:116), [file.cljc:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:279).\\n- No legacy decoder, printer alias, or old-address fallback was found.\\n\\nVerification: focused JVM suite passed 80 tests / 3,200 assertions; clj-kondo reported 0 errors and 0 warnings; cljstyle passed. No files were edited.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.md:513:  order-normalized printer, its three residuals (symbol metadata not\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128:It continues to return the UTF-8 bytes of the current order-normalized, metadata-aware print encoding.\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505:The algorithm-registry epic lands first over the current order-normalized print encoder.\\ntest/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn:32:;; order-normalized print encoder; regenerated 2026-09-24 along with every\\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:152 len=168\\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:153 len=168\\ndocs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md:154 len=168\\ndocs/agents/team.md:129 len=170\\ndocs/agents/team.md:130 len=170\\ndocs/agents/team.md:131 len=170\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6706 nonASCII len=101\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6710 len=115\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6725 len=81\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6739 len=88\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6740 len=89\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6741 len=89\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6749 len=118\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6750 len=127\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6752 len=120\\ndocs/orchestrator-log.md:6758 nonASCII \\nsrc/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:3 nonASCII \\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501 len=82\\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:314 len=88\\ntest/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc:294 len=82\\ntest/dao/jing/remote_test.cljc:147 len=81\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790264589986-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:90:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_45\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba test/resources/dao/jing/digest-table.edn | sed -n '24,38p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc | sed -n '1,33p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md | sed -n '500,528p'; git diff --name-only -- src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc; git diff --unified=0 -- test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc | sed -n '1,90p'; rg -n 'copy-bytes|byte-payload\\\\\\\\?|defn bytes=' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | head -18\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    24\\t;; fixture readers agree on what bytes to feed their provider before any\\n    25\\t;; comparison happens. A hex string too long for one line is written as a\\n    26\\t;; vector of string chunks, concatenated by the reader.\\n    27\\t;;\\n    28\\t;; Canonical-Clojure-data-structure cases were produced by hashing the\\n    29\\t;; exact bytes `dao.jing/canonical-bytes` returns for the given value:\\n    30\\t;; the canonical CBOR payload of docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, as\\n    31\\t;; `dao.jing.cbor/encode` writes it (the clean break from the earlier\\n    32\\t;; order-normalized print encoder; regenerated 2026-09-24 along with every\\n    33\\t;; other DaoJing address fixture, per that plan's clean-break ruling).\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t{:providers {:jvm  \\\"io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 1.3\\\"\\n    36\\t             :cljs \\\"@noble/hashes 2.4.0\\\"\\n    37\\t             :cljd \\\"blake3_dart 1.0.0\\\"}\\n    38\\t\\n     1\\t(ns dao.data.btree.storage\\n     2\\t  \\\"IStorage adapters over dao.jing content-store handles (docs/design/dao.data.btree.md\\n     3\\t   §5.1, §5.4). A handle is the plain-data byte store carrying\\n     4\\t   :put-bytes-fn, :get-bytes-fn, and :close-fn (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\\n     5\\t   Materialization rule); addresses are derived from payloads by\\n     6\\t   dao.jing/materialize!, never supplied by this layer. Two adapters, one\\n     7\\t   error taxonomy:\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t   - KVStorage (kv-storage): the sync rule-1 adapter. Absence is\\n    10\\t     authoritative: a missing blob throws \\\\\\\"missing index segment\\\\\\\".\\n    11\\t     Optional same-host integrity verification (§5.2): rehash the fetched\\n    12\\t     blob against its content address; mismatch throws \\\\\\\"corrupt index\\n    13\\t     segment\\\\\\\". Off by default — dao.jing's print-based content hash is not\\n    14\\t     host-stable, so cross-host reads must not verify (dao.jing.md, Current\\n    15\\t     Scope).\\n    16\\t   - HydrationStorage (hydration-storage): the §5.4 hydration-cache\\n    17\\t     adapter for async-only backends. Reads answer only from the cache;\\n    18\\t     any miss throws \\\\\\\"unhydrated segment\\\\\\\" — the cache cannot distinguish\\n    19\\t     absent from not-yet-fetched without blocking on the backend. hydrate!\\n    20\\t     copies the reachable blob graph from the source into the cache. The\\n    21\\t     source is either a sync content handle or an async one\\n    22\\t     (dao.jing.remote.async/async-content); over an async source the\\n    23\\t     non-blocking hydrate-async and store-tree-async are the only way in\\n    24\\t     and out, and the sync store path throws.\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\t   dao.data.btree itself stays storage-agnostic; this namespace is the one\\n    27\\t   place the tree meets dao.jing (Decision 1: no new storage protocol —\\n    28\\t   everything below is materialize!/get, plus segment-matches? for §5.2\\n    29\\t   verification).\\\"\\n    30\\t  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\\\"dart:async\\\" :as async]])\\n    31\\t            [dao.data.btree :as bt]\\n    32\\t            [dao.jing :as jing]))\\n    33\\t\\n   500\\t\\n   501\\tAny provider version or replacement that changes a pinned digest is rejected as nonconforming to the registered algorithm.\\n   502\\t\\n   503\\t## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\\n   504\\t\\n   505\\tThe algorithm-registry epic lands first over the current order-normalized print encoder.\\n   506\\t\\n   507\\tIt establishes:\\n   508\\t\\n   509\\t- the flat multihash address grammar;\\n   510\\t- permanent BLAKE3 and SHA-256 entries;\\n   511\\t- BLAKE3 as the minting default;\\n   512\\t- explicit SHA-256 minting;\\n   513\\t- algorithm-directed verification; and\\n   514\\t- algorithm-preserving copy paths.\\n   515\\t\\n   516\\tCanonical CBOR remains a separate clean-break epic. A canonical codec already exists in `dao.jing.cbor`; its byte contract is frozen by `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json` and is currently used for encoded comparison rather than addressing.\\n   517\\t\\n   518\\tWhen CBOR lands, it replaces the implementation of `canonical-bytes` outright. The algorithm registry remains unchanged:\\n   519\\t\\n   520\\t- BLAKE3 remains the default;\\n   521\\t- SHA-256 remains explicitly selectable;\\n   522\\t- the address grammar remains `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>`;\\n   523\\t- every digest and every DaoJing address is regenerated from CBOR bytes;\\n   524\\t- development stores, fixtures, manifests, ASTs, and continuations are rebuilt together; and\\n   525\\t- no printer-address reader, alias, graph migration, or encoding negotiation is introduced.\\n   526\\t\\n   527\\tThe existing `dao.jing.cbor.md` \\\"Addressing and clean break\\\" ruling remains authoritative. This document changes its SHA-only algorithm assumption but does not alter its clean-break policy.\\n   528\\t\\ndiff --git a/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc b/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc\\nindex f125db49..88d5fc29 100644\\n--- a/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc\\n+++ b/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc\\n@@ -82,12 +82,12 @@\\n-            :put-content-fn (fn [address payload]\\n-                              (let [result (if (contains? @store address)\\n-                                             :present\\n-                                             (do (swap! store assoc address payload)\\n-                                                 :inserted))]\\n-                                (note! [:put address result])\\n-                                result))\\n-            :get-content-fn (fn [address not-found]\\n-                              (note! [:get address])\\n-                              (if (contains? @store address)\\n-                                (get @store address)\\n-                                not-found))))))\\n+            :put-bytes-fn (fn [address bs]\\n+                            (let [result (if (contains? @store address)\\n+                                           :present\\n+                                           (do (swap! store assoc address bs)\\n+                                               :inserted))]\\n+                              (note! [:put address result])\\n+                              result))\\n+            :get-bytes-fn (fn [address not-found]\\n+                     