 {:ok false})\\n   127\\t           (catch Exception _ {:ok false})))\\n   128\\t       ;; :found travels explicitly on both reads: nil is a legal stored\\n   129\\t       ;; value, so presence cannot be inferred from :v\\n   130\\t       :fetch-content\\n   131\\t       (let [bs (if (jing/segment-address? (:address msg))\\n   132\\t                  ((:get-bytes-fn local) (:address msg) missing)\\n   246\\t                                 {:algorithm algo})))\\n   247\\t         addr (keyword \\\"segment\\\"\\n   248\\t                       (str (:address-id reg) \\\"-\\\" (jing/digest-bytes algo bs)))]\\n   249\\t     (submit state put-content-op [addr (jing/bytes->base64 bs)] true))))\\n   250\\t\\n   251\\t\\n   252\\t(defn- accepted-bytes\\n   253\\t  \\\"The canonical bytes a `:jing/get-content` envelope carries for address\\n   254\\t   as Base64 text, or nil when the text is not strict Base64 or the bytes\\n   255\\t   do not hash to the address.\\\"\\n   256\\t  [address b64]\\n   257\\t  (try (let [bs (jing/base64->bytes b64)]\\n   258\\t         (when (jing/segment-bytes-match? address bs) bs))\\n   259\\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\\n   260\\t         nil)))\\n   261\\t\\n   262\\t\\n   263\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   264\\t;; Completion decode\\n   290\\t            (if (#{:inserted :present} value)\\n   291\\t              {:id id :op op :address (first (:dao.stream.rpc/args completion))\\n   292\\t               :result value}\\n   293\\t              {:id id :op op :error (malformed-response op)})\\n   294\\t\\n   295\\t            ;; a found value arrives as Base64 of its canonical bytes; the\\n   296\\t            ;; bytes must hash to the requested address and decode as one\\n   297\\t            ;; canonical payload before a value is published\\n   298\\t            (= get-content-op op)\\n   299\\t            (if (presence-envelope? value)\\n   300\\t              (if (:found? value)\\n   301\\t                (let [address (first (:dao.stream.rpc/args completion))\\n   302\\t                      bs (accepted-bytes address (:value value))\\n   303\\t                      decoded (when bs\\n   304\\t                                (try (jing/segment-value address bs)\\n   305\\t                                     (catch #?(:cljd Object\\n   306\\t                                               :clj Throwable\\n   307\\t                                               :cljs :default)\\n   308\\t                                            _\\n   309\\t                                       ::refused)))]\\n   310\\t                  (if (or (nil? bs) (identical? ::refused decoded))\\n   311\\t                    {:id id :op op\\n   124\\t- Verify every retrieved payload against its requested address before\\n   125\\t  decoding exactly one supported value. Canonicality verification (decode,\\n   126\\t  re-encode, byte-compare) occurs exactly once at each ingress of bytes Jing\\n   127\\t  did not encode itself: remote/DHT receipt and file replay acceptance,\\n   128\\t  before storage, caching, or exposure. Ordinary reads of accepted snapshots\\n   129\\t  hash-verify and decode without re-encoding. Validation belongs to the\\n   130\\t  shared codec/wrapper at those boundaries, not to backend value handlers;\\n   131\\t  immutable local forwarding does not repeat ingress validation. Missing\\n   132\\t  content remains distinct from stored nil, which has a CBOR representation.\\n   133\\t- Store immutable snapshots. Memory backends copy byte arrays on insertion\\n   134\\t  and retrieval so caller mutation cannot change previously addressed\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790264589986-reviewer-daojing-cbor-swap-fixes-r2.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:98:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_50\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-24 15:55:32 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-24 22:55:32 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\nThe five prior findings are now:\\n\\n1. **CLOSED** — The memory handle exposes closures rather than `:state`, returns byte copies, and verifies `entries` before decoding ([mem.cljc:96](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:96), [mem.cljc:132](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:132), [mem.cljc:145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:145)). A separate concurrent-mutation issue remains below.\\n2. **CLOSED** — `materialize!` encodes the payload once, hashes those bytes, and passes them to `:put-bytes-fn` ([jing.cljc:512](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:512)).\\n3. **CLOSED** — `dao.jing.md` now specifies CBOR, byte stores, and `[digest payload-bytes]` frames ([dao.jing.md:193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:193), [dao.jing.md:411](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:411)). The separate hash-registry design remains stale.\\n4. **PARTIALLY CLOSED** — Replay tests now cover the requested CBOR refusal classes and unchanged-file rejection ([file_test.cljc:278](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:278)). Memory tests cover caller-array isolation ([mem_test.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:234)); the file tests cover disk-byte corruption, but not caller input/output array mutation ([file_test.cljc:326](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326)).\\n5. **CLOSED for the three previously named fixtures** — The current `psset_fixtures.cljc`, `hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`, and `digest-table.edn` contain only ASCII and lines of at most 80 columns. The wider changed-file hygiene gate still fails.\\n\\nActionable findings:\\n\\nP1 | [src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1176) | The Node run’s durable round-trip errors when `:yin.debruijn/value` contains decoded `#dao.jing/float64 1.5`; the canonical classifier accepts numbers but not this carrier ([debruijn.cljc:273](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:273), [test-cljs.log:311](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:311)). Its second failure compares a decoded carrier to bare `2.5` ([pipeline_test.cljc:585](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc:585), [test-cljs.log:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/target/test-cljs.log:316)). The codec *requires* a JS carrier on decode ([dao.jing.cbor.md:375](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:375)); these are unresolved consumer/test defects, not an accepted passing limitation. | Make the VM’s canonical-value and numeric paths carrier-aware, compare portable content in the test, and require a green Node suite.\\n\\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:51) | Both byte-store puts verify a caller-owned mutable array *before* copying it ([file.cljc:348](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:348)). A concurrent caller mutation between validation and copy can store or append bytes that no longer hash to the address; duplicate checks also compare the original array ([mem.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:60), [file.cljc:360](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:360)). | Copy first, then validate, frame, and compare only the snapshot.\\n\\nP2 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1536) | `decode` re-encodes every value. Consequently ordinary accepted-snapshot reads through `segment-value` re-encode too ([jing.cljc:470](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:470)), contrary to the explicit read-path contract ([dao.jing.cbor.md:124](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:124)). | Separate strict ingress canonicality validation from trusted-snapshot decoding while retaining address-hash verification on reads.\\n\\nP2 | [docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:128) | This governing document still says `canonical-bytes` returns order-normalized print bytes and that CBOR is future work ([dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md:505)); the implementation directly calls `cbor/encode` ([jing.cljc:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:279)). | Update the registry document to describe the completed clean break and current digest inputs.\\n\\nP3 | [test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/file_test.cljc:326) | The new mutation test changes bytes on disk; it does not exercise mutation of the byte array passed to, or returned from, the file handle, unlike the memory tests ([mem_test.cljc:234](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/jing/mem_test.cljc:234)). | Add file-handle input and output array mutation-isolation tests, including a reopen check.\\n\\nP3 | [src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1501) | An added line is 82 columns. Across all 40 tracked changed files, the current-file scan finds non-ASCII in 22 files and over-80-column lines in 25; added-line examples also occur at [storage.cljc:3](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc:3) and [remote/step.cljc:314](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/remote/step.cljc:314). Many wider-file violations predate these edits, but the requested global gate is not clean. | Reflow and ASCII-normalize the changed files, starting with added violations.\\n\\nReview was read-only. I relied on the retained Node log and static checks; I did not rerun the suites. The fresh JVM result supplied in the brief does not resolve the Node failures.\\n\\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:17:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790243232166-vm-engineer-ucf-phase1.prompt.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-09-24 09:47:12 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-09-24 16:47:12 +0700\\nCoding-Agent: claude\\nSession-ID: 5703a677-1239-493a-b3cc-67468505a580\\n\\n# Task: UCF Phase 1 — Canonical Instruction Vector & Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata\\n\\nRole: Yin.VM Runtime Engineer\\n\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: claude-sonnet-5 | Assigned: 2026-09-24 16:47:12 +0700 | Status: active | Rationale: Phase 1 implementation of Universal Continuation Format (UCF) per yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md and team.md\\n\\nImplement UCF Phase 1 in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf`.\\n\\nRead first:\\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (specifically §7.1, §7.2, §7.3, §7.4, and §7.11)\\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` (§1–§4, opcode table, well-formedness rules)\\n- `docs/design/datom.world.md`\\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc`\\n- `src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc`\\n\\nContext & Layering:\\nCBOR encoding is an implementation detail inside `dao.jing`. UCF operates over pure data structures and content addresses via `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` and must not touch or depend on CBOR serialization specifics.\\n\\nScope & Acceptance Criteria for Phase 1:\\n1. §7.3 Canonical Instruction Vector:\\n   - Implement `batch->canonical-instruction-vector`:\\n     Accepts a well-formed semantic code batch, applies the loader's resolved interpretation (index-batch collapse, last-value-wins), and produces the positional instruction tuple vector `[[:closure [x] 6] [:push] [:const 10] ...]`.\\n   - PC is index; no entity IDs or attribute keywords in tuples.\\n   - Saturation: default operands are materialized per §7.3.2.\\n   - Non-canonicalizable check: refuse with `:yin.k/non-portable` and `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable` on unknown opcodes or invalid tuple shapes.\\n   - Code identity: compute `:yin.code/hash` via `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`.\\n   - Contract stamp: include the execution contract stamp identifying the tuple grammar and execution semantics per §7.3.3.\\n2. §7.4 Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:\\n   - Identify parking transitions across the 12 semantic VM transitions (including effect-producing `:call`s).\\n   - Implement static safepoint metadata derivation:\\n     Separate static properties derivable from code (stack effect, lexically required environment names) from dynamic frame activation state (absolute depth, captured bindings).\\n3. Test Coverage:\\n   - Add comprehensive unit tests in `test/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc` covering:\\n     - Canonical instruction vector generation across semantic test batches.\\n     - Equivalence laws: alpha-equivalent and defaulted variants produce identical instruction vectors and identical addresses.\\n     - Distinction laws: semantically differing programs produce distinct vectors and addresses.\\n     - Safepoint metadata derivation and stack/env requirement accuracy.\\n4. Invariants & Code Hygiene:\\n   - Pure ASCII only.\\n   - Lines strictly <= 80 columns.\\n   - Zero linter errors or warnings (`clj -M:kondo`).\\n   - Clean `cljstyle check`.\\n   - Pass `bb test:clj`, `bb test:cljs`, `bb test:cljd` in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf`.\\n\\nWork only in named files. If a required dependency demands expansion, stop and report before editing. Preserve unrelated changes, do not weaken tests, and preserve CESK and execution-parity invariants.\\n\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\n\\nReport changed files, exact test/check outcomes, unresolved concerns, and any incomplete work. Do not claim edits or tests that did not occur.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:19:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t(ns yin.vm.ucf\\n     2\\t  \\\"Universal Continuation Format, Phase 1: code identity and safepoints\\n     3\\t   (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.3, S7.4).\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t   Two static halves of the format live here, both pure functions over\\n     6\\t   data:\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\t   - S7.3 code identity. `batch->canonical-instruction-vector` reads a\\n     9\\t     well-formed `:yin.code/*` segment batch through the loader's resolved\\n    10\\t     interpretation (index-batch collapse, last value wins) and produces\\n    11\\t     the positional instruction tuple vector -- pc is the index, each tuple\\n    12\\t     is its mnemonic plus exactly the S2.4 operands in table order, every\\n    13\\t     ref a resolved pc, every defaulted operand materialized. The address\\n    14\\t     of that vector is `(dao.jing/segment-key v)`; the execution contract\\n    15\\t     it is interpreted under is `contract-stamp`. `canonicalize` bundles\\n    16\\t     the three into one S7.9 outcome map.\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t   - S7.4 safepoints. `safepoints` derives, from the canonical vector\\n    19\\t     alone, the static half of the safepoint map a foreign engine\\n    20\\t     publishes: the parking transitions, their canonical resume pcs, the\\n    21\\t     stack effect across the parking instruction, and the names the code\\n    22\\t     reachable from the resume pc can read from E. The dynamic half --\\n    23\\t     absolute depth, captured bindings, activation bases -- is never\\n    24\\t     derived: `activation-state` reads it off a frame, which is the only\\n    25\\t     place it exists.\\n    26\\t\\n    27\\t   Content addressing is delegated entirely to `dao.jing`; nothing here\\n    28\\t   knows how a vector is serialized.\\\"\\n    29\\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\\n    30\\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n    31\\t            [yin.vm.code :as code]))\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t\\n    34\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    35\\t;; The execution contract (S7.3.3)\\n    36\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    37\\t\\n    38\\t(def contract-stamp\\n    39\\t  \\\"The complete execution contract a canonical vector is interpreted under:\\n    40\\t   `:yin.code/contract` names the published revision of the tuple grammar,\\n    41\\t   S2.6 rules, last-value-wins resolution, opcode table, transitions,\\n    42\\t   resolution precedence, effect outcomes and scheduler\\n    43\\t   (`yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4); `:yin.k/version` is this envelope's own\\n    44\\t   version. A resumer on another revision answers `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`\\n    45\\t   before lowering; a code index is keyed per stamp.\\\"\\n    46\\t  {:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\",\\n    47\\t   :yin.k/version 0})\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    51\\t;; The canonical instruction vector (S7.3.2)\\n    52\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t(def ^:private segment-attrs\\n    55\\t  \\\"Segment-entity attributes the canonical form folds away or excludes:\\n    56\\t   the type and length are implied by the vector, provenance and the hash\\n    57\\t   itself are excluded. Anything else on the segment entity is a fact the\\n    58\\t   vector has no slot for.\\\"\\n    59\\t  #{:yin.code/type :yin.code/length :yin.code/derived-from :yin.code/hash})\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t\\n    62\\t(def ^:private structural-attrs\\n    63\\t  \\\"Instruction attributes that position and the mnemonic replace.\\\"\\n    64\\t  #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op})\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\t(def ^:private excluded-instruction-attrs\\n    68\\t  \\\"Instruction attributes excluded from identity: provenance.\\\"\\n    69\\t  #{:yin.code/source})\\n    70\\t\\n    71\\t\\n    72\\t(def ^:private saturation-defaults\\n    73\\t  \\\"The loader's defaults, materialized so an omitted operand and a stated\\n    74\\t   one canonicalize identically (S7.3.2, `yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4).\\\"\\n    75\\t  {[:gensym :yin.code/prefix] \\\"id\\\",\\n    76\\t   [:stream-make :yin.code/buffer] vm/default-stream-capacity,\\n    77\\t   [:ffi-call :yin.code/argc] 0,\\n    78\\t   [:call :yin.code/tail?] false})\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\t\\n    81\\t(defn- index-batch\\n    82\\t  \\\"Entity ids in order of first appearance, and each entity's attribute\\n    83\\t   map. A repeated single-valued attribute keeps its last value in datom\\n    84\\t   order -- the resolved interpretation the loader executes (S7.3.2).\\\"\\n    85\\t  [datoms]\\n    86\\t  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\\n    87\\t            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\\n    88\\t             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\\n    89\\t          [[] {}]\\n    90\\t          datoms))\\n    91\\t\\n    92\\t\\n    93\\t(defn refusal\\n    94\\t  \\\"The S7.9 `:yin.k/non-portable` outcome for a batch that has no canonical\\n    95\\t   vector, naming the offending place (`:yin.k/path`) and the defect.\\\"\\n    96\\t  [path defect hint]\\n    97\\t  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable,\\n    98\\t   :yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable,\\n    99\\t   :yin.k/path path,\\n   100\\t   :yin.k/defect defect,\\n   101\\t   :yin.k/hint hint})\\n   102\\t\\n   103\\t\\n   104\\t(defn- refuse\\n   105\\t  [path defect hint]\\n   106\\t  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Cannot canonicalize segment: \\\" hint)\\n   107\\t                  (refusal path defect hint))))\\n   108\\t\\n   109\\t\\n   110\\t(defn- resolved-batch\\n   111\\t  \\\"The loader's reading of a batch: `{:seg :attrs :instructions}` with\\n   112\\t   instructions in pc order. A batch that is not well formed (S2.6) is not\\n   113\\t   a segment, and is refused with the loader's own defect.\\\"\\n   114\\t  [datoms]\\n   115\\t  (when-let [defect (code/well-formed? datoms)]\\n   116\\t    (refuse [:entity (:entity defect)]\\n   117\\t            defect\\n   118\\t            (str \\\"the batch is not well formed: \\\" (name (:rule defect)))))\\n   119\\t  (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\\n   120\\t        seg (some #(when (= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) %)\\n   121\\t                  order)\\n   122\\t        instructions (filterv (fn [e]\\n   123\\t                                (and (not= seg e)\\n   124\\t                                     (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\\n   125\\t                                           structural-attrs)))\\n   126\\t                              order)]\\n   127\\t    {:seg seg, :attrs attrs, :instructions instructions}))\\n   128\\t\\n   129\\t\\n   130\\t(defn- check-segment-entity\\n   131\\t  [seg attrs]\\n   132\\t  (let [extra (remove segment-attrs (keys (get attrs seg)))]\\n   133\\t    (when (seq extra)\\n   134\\t      (refuse [:entity seg]\\n   135\\t              {:rule :segment-attribute, :entity seg, :attrs (vec extra)}\\n   136\\t              \\\"the segment entity carries an attribute with no slot\\\"))))\\n   137\\t\\n   138\\t\\n   139\\t(defn- canonical-tuple\\n   140\\t  \\\"One instruction's tuple: the mnemonic, then its S2.4 operands in table\\n   141\\t   order, refs resolved to pcs and defaults materialized. Refused when the\\n   142\\t   mnemonic has no tuple or the instruction carries an operand its tuple\\n   143\\t   has no slot for.\\\"\\n   144\\t  [pc-of pc e ia]\\n   145\\t  (let [mnem (:yin.code/op ia)\\n   146\\t        operands (get code/vector-operand-table mnem)]\\n   147\\t    (when-not operands\\n   148\\t      (refuse [:pc pc]\\n   149\\t              {:rule :mnemonic, :pc pc, :entity e, :op mnem}\\n   150\\t              \\\"the mnemonic is outside the S2.4 table\\\"))\\n   151\\t    (let [slots (into #{} (map first) operands)\\n   152\\t          extra (remove (fn [a]\\n   153\\t                          (or (structural-attrs a)\\n   154\\t                              (excluded-instruction-attrs a)\\n   155\\t                              (slots a)))\\n   156\\t                        (keys ia))]\\n   157\\t      (when (seq extra)\\n   158\\t        (refuse [:pc pc]\\n   159\\t                {:rule :operand-slot, :pc pc, :entity e, :attrs (vec extra)}\\n   160\\t                \\\"the instruction carries an operand its tuple has no slot for\\\"))\\n   161\\t      (into [mnem]\\n   162\\t            (map (fn [[a kind]]\\n   163\\t                   (let [v (get ia a)]\\n   164\\t                     (cond\\n   165\\t                       (= :pc kind)\\n   166\\t                       (or (get pc-of v)\\n   167\\t                           (refuse [:pc pc]\\n   168\\t                                   {:rule :dangling-target, :pc pc,\\n   169\\t                                    :entity e, :ref v}\\n   170\\t                                   \\\"a ref resolves to no instruction\\\"))\\n   171\\t                       (nil? v) (get saturation-defaults [mnem a])\\n   172\\t                       :else v))))\\n   173\\t            operands))))\\n   174\\t\\n   175\\t\\n   176\\t(defn batch->canonical-instruction-vector\\n   177\\t  \\\"The canonical instruction vector (S7.3.2) of one `:yin.code/*` segment\\n   178\\t   batch, or a thrown `:yin.k/non-portable` refusal (`refusal`, kind\\n   179\\t   `:non-canonicalizable`) when the batch has none.\\n   180\\t\\n   181\\t   The batch is read through the loader's resolved interpretation, so two\\n   182\\t   batches that execute alike canonicalize alike: entity ids, datom order\\n   183\\t   within an entity, provenance, and omitted defaulted operands leave the\\n   184\\t   vector unchanged; a repeated attribute keeps its last value. The result\\n   185\\t   is checked against the S7.5 tuple grammar before it is returned, so a\\n   186\\t   vector this function yields is always one `load-vector` accepts.\\\"\\n   187\\t  [datoms]\\n   188\\t  (let [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]} (resolved-batch datoms)\\n   189\\t        pc-of (zipmap instructions (range))]\\n   190\\t    (check-segment-entity seg attrs)\\n   191\\t    (let [v (into []\\n   192\\t                  (map-indexed (fn [pc e]\\n   193\\t                                 (canonical-tuple pc-of pc e (get attrs e))))\\n   194\\t                  instructions)]\\n   195\\t      (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\\n   196\\t        (refuse [:pc (:pc defect)]\\n   197\\t                defect\\n   198\\t                (str \\\"the resolved tuple is not admissible: \\\"\\n   199\\t                     (name (:rule defect)))))\\n   200\\t      v)))\\n   201\\t\\n   202\\t\\n   203\\t(defn code-address\\n   204\\t  \\\"`:yin.code/hash` of a canonical vector: its `dao.jing` segment address.\\n   205\\t   UCF mints no second addressing scheme.\\\"\\n   206\\t  [v]\\n   207\\t  (jing/segment-key v))\\n   208\\t\\n   209\\t\\n   210\\t(defn canonicalize\\n   211\\t  \\\"Code identity as one S7.9 outcome. On success\\n   212\\t   `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/ok, :yin.code/vector v, :yin.code/hash address,\\n   213\\t   :yin.k/contract contract-stamp}`; otherwise the `refusal` map the\\n   214\\t   vector function would have thrown.\\\"\\n   215\\t  [datoms]\\n   216\\t  (try\\n   217\\t    (let [v (batch->canonical-instruction-vector datoms)]\\n   218\\t      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/ok,\\n   219\\t       :yin.code/vector v,\\n   220\\t       :yin.code/hash (code-address v),\\n   221\\t       :yin.k/contract contract-stamp})\\n   222\\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n   223\\t      (let [data (ex-data e)]\\n   224\\t        (if (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status data))\\n   225\\t          data\\n   226\\t          (throw e))))))\\n   227\\t\\n   228\\t\\n   229\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   230\\t;; Safepoints (S7.4)\\n   231\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   232\\t\\n   233\\t(def transitions\\n   234\\t  \\\"Every S2.4 mnemonic classified by what its S4.2 transition does to\\n   235\\t   control. `:step` continues at pc+1; `:jump`, `:branch` and `:transfer`\\n   236\\t   move control (a `:resume` restores a parked configuration, so its\\n   237\\t   successor is not pc+1); `:terminal` leaves no successor; `:parking` may\\n   238\\t   leave the machine parked with a canonical configuration at pc+1 -- the\\n   239\\t   safepoints of S7.4.1. `:call` is parking because the operator may be a\\n   240\\t   primitive whose result is a blocking effect; `:current-continuation`\\n   241\\t   is a step, not a safepoint (it reifies and continues).\\\"\\n   242\\t  {:const :step,\\n   243\\t   :var :step,\\n   244\\t   :closure :step,\\n   245\\t   :push :step,\\n   246\\t   :gensym :step,\\n   247\\t   :store-get :step,\\n   248\\t   :store-put :step,\\n   249\\t   :stream-make :step,\\n   250\\t   :stream-cursor :step,\\n   251\\t   :stream-close :step,\\n   252\\t   :current-continuation :step,\\n   253\\t   :jump :jump,\\n   254\\t   :branch-false :branch,\\n   255\\t   :resume :transfer,\\n   256\\t   :return :terminal,\\n   257\\t   :halt :terminal,\\n   258\\t   :park :parking,\\n   259\\t   :stream-next :parking,\\n   260\\t   :stream-put :parking,\\n   261\\t   :ffi-call :parking,\\n   262\\t   :call :parking})\\n   263\\t\\n   264\\t\\n   265\\t(def ^:private parking-reasons\\n   266\\t  \\\"S7.4.1's safepoint kinds, by parking mnemonic. An `:ffi-call` parks\\n   267\\t   under two reasons -- sent, or retained while the request is in hand --\\n   268\\t   at one safepoint.\\\"\\n   269\\t  {:park [:park],\\n   270\\t   :stream-next [:next],\\n   271\\t   :stream-put [:put],\\n   272\\t   :ffi-call [:ffi :ffi-request],\\n   273\\t   :call [:call-effect]})\\n   274\\t\\n   275\\t\\n   276\\t(defn stack-effect\\n   277\\t  \\\"Delta |St| across one parking instruction, from the tuple alone\\n   278\\t   (S7.4.1's `Stack in frame` column): a park and a blocked read leave St;\\n   279\\t   a blocked write has popped its target; an FFI call its arguments; an\\n   280\\t   effectful call its operator and arguments.\\\"\\n   281\\t  [t]\\n   282\\t  (case (nth t 0)\\n   283\\t    :park 0\\n   284\\t    :stream-next 0\\n   285\\t    :stream-put -1\\n   286\\t    :ffi-call (- (nth t 2))\\n   287\\t    :call (- (inc (nth t 1)))\\n   288\\t    nil))\\n   289\\t\\n   290\\t\\n   291\\t(defn- successors\\n   292\\t  \\\"The pcs control may reach next from `pc`, with the names bound on the\\n   293\\t   way: a closure's body is entered with its params bound, everything else\\n   294\\t   keeps the current binding set.\\\"\\n   295\\t  [v pc bound]\\n   296\\t  (let [t (nth v pc)\\n   297\\t        mnem (nth t 0)]\\n   298\\t    (case (get transitions mnem)\\n   299\\t      :step (if (= :closure mnem)\\n   300\\t              [[(inc pc) bound] [(nth t 2) (into bound (nth t 1))]]\\n   301\\t              [[(inc pc) bound]])\\n   302\\t      :parking [[(inc pc) bound]]\\n   303\\t      :jump [[(nth t 1) bound]]\\n   304\\t      :branch [[(inc pc) bound] [(nth t 1) bound]]\\n   305\\t      [])))\\n   306\\t\\n   307\\t\\n   308\\t(defn lexically-required\\n   309\\t  \\\"The names the code reachable from `pc` reads from E, from the vector\\n   310\\t   alone (S7.4.2): every `:var` name on a control path from `pc` --\\n   311\\t   through fall-through, jumps, branches, and the bodies of closures\\n   312\\t   created on the way -- minus the names those closures bind. Callees are\\n   313\\t   values, not code reachable here: a closure already on the stack carries\\n   314\\t   its own captured environment, which is the frame's business. Sorted by\\n   315\\t   name so the result is a stable value.\\\"\\n   316\\t  [v pc]\\n   317\\t  (loop [work [[pc #{}]]\\n   318\\t         seen #{}\\n   319\\t         names #{}]\\n   320\\t    (if-let [[p bound :as item] (peek work)]\\n   321\\t      (let [work (pop work)]\\n   322\\t        (if (contains? seen item)\\n   323\\t          (recur work seen names)\\n   324\\t          (let [t (nth v p)\\n   325\\t                names (if (and (= :var (nth t 0))\\n   326\\t                               (not (contains? bound (nth t 1))))\\n   327\\t                        (conj names (nth t 1))\\n   328\\t                        names)]\\n   329\\t            (recur (into work (successors v p bound))\\n   330\\t                   (conj seen item)\\n   331\\t                   names))))\\n   332\\t      (vec (sort-by str names)))))\\n   333\\t\\n   334\\t\\n   335\\t(defn safepoints\\n   336\\t  \\\"The static safepoint map of one canonical vector (S7.4.2): one entry per\\n   337\\t   parking instruction, each carrying only what the vector alone fixes --\\n   338\\t   the parking pc, the canonical resume pc (pc+1, always inside the segment\\n   339\\t   by S2.6), the reasons under which the machine parks there, the stack\\n   340\\t   effect across the instruction, and the lexically required names at the\\n   341\\t   resume pc. Absolute depth, activation bases, captured environments and\\n   342\\t   physical layouts are not here: they ride in the frame\\n   343\\t   (`activation-state`).\\\"\\n   344\\t  [v]\\n   345\\t  (into []\\n   346\\t        (keep-indexed\\n   347\\t          (fn [pc t]\\n   348\\t            (when (= :parking (get transitions (nth t 0)))\\n   349\\t              {:yin.safepoint/at pc,\\n   350\\t               :yin.safepoint/pc (inc pc),\\n   351\\t               :yin.safepoint/reasons (get parking-reasons (nth t 0)),\\n   352\\t               :yin.safepoint/stack-effect (stack-effect t),\\n   353\\t               :yin.safepoint/lexically-required\\n   354\\t               (lexically-required v (inc pc))})))\\n   355\\t        v))\\n   356\\t\\n   357\\t\\n   358\\t(defn safepoint-table\\n   359\\t  \\\"`safepoints` published per segment, keyed by the code's address and the\\n   360\\t   engine profile that derived it (the reference machine by default).\\\"\\n   361\\t  ([v] (safepoint-table v :yin.vm.semantic/reference))\\n   362\\t  ([v engine]\\n   363\\t   {:yin.safepoint/segment (code-address v),\\n   364\\t    :yin.safepoint/engine engine,\\n   365\\t    :yin.safepoint/safepoints (safepoints v)}))\\n   366\\t\\n   367\\t\\n   368\\t(defn safepoint-at\\n   369\\t  \\\"The static safepoint entry whose canonical resume pc is `pc`, or the\\n   370\\t   S7.9 `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` outcome naming the pc.\\\"\\n   371\\t  [v pc]\\n   372\\t  (or (some #(when (= pc (:yin.safepoint/pc %)) %) (safepoints v))\\n   373\\t      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-at-safepoint, :yin.k/pc pc}))\\n   374\\t\\n   375\\t\\n   376\\t(defn activation-state\\n   377\\t  \\\"The dynamic half of a parked configuration, read off a frame -- a wait\\n   378\\t   entry or parked record `{segment pc env stack k}` -- never derived from\\n   379\\t   code: the absolute operand-stack depth, the names E actually binds, and\\n   380\\t   each return frame's activation base.\\\"\\n   381\\t  [frame]\\n   382\\t  {:yin.k/depth (count (:stack frame)),\\n   383\\t   :yin.k/bound (vec (sort-by str (keys (:env frame)))),\\n   384\\t   :yin.k/stack-bases (mapv :stack-base (:k frame))})\\n   385\\t\\n   386\\t\\n   387\\t(defn unsatisfied-names\\n   388\\t  \\\"The lexically required names of a static safepoint that the frame's\\n   389\\t   environment does not bind and that `resolvable` (store, primitives,\\n   390\\t   modules -- the rest of `resolve-var`'s order) does not answer: the\\n   391\\t   conformance check that a frame carries what its code will read.\\\"\\n   392\\t  [safepoint frame resolvable]\\n   393\\t  (vec (remove (fn [n]\\n   394\\t                 (or (contains? (:env frame) n) (resolvable n)))\\n   395\\t               (:yin.safepoint/lexically-required safepoint))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba test/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t(ns yin.vm.ucf-test\\n     2\\t  \\\"UCF Phase 1 (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.3,\\n     3\\t   S7.4): the canonical instruction vector, its address and contract stamp,\\n     4\\t   and the static safepoint map -- checked against the reference machine.\\n     5\\t\\n     6\\t   Fixtures are S2.7-shaped batches assembled by hand, as in\\n     7\\t   `yin.vm.semantic-test`, so the resolved interpretation under test is the\\n     8\\t   loader's own.\\\"\\n     9\\t  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n    10\\t            [dao.datom :as datom]\\n    11\\t            [dao.jing :as jing]\\n    12\\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\\n    13\\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n    14\\t            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic]\\n    15\\t            [yin.vm.test-utils :as tu]\\n    16\\t            [yin.vm.ucf :as ucf]))\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    20\\t;; Hand assembly\\n    21\\t;; =============================================================================\\n    22\\t\\n    23\\t(def ^:private seg -1)\\n    24\\t\\n    25\\t\\n    26\\t(defn- eid\\n    27\\t  [pc]\\n    28\\t  (- -10 pc))\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\t(defn- entity-datoms\\n    32\\t  [e attr-values]\\n    33\\t  (mapv (fn [[a v]] [e a v 0 datom/default-op]) attr-values))\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t(defn- segment\\n    37\\t  ([length] (segment length []))\\n    38\\t  ([length extra]\\n    39\\t   (entity-datoms seg (into [[:yin.code/type :segment]\\n    40\\t                             [:yin.code/length length]]\\n    41\\t                            extra))))\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\t(defn- instruction*\\n    45\\t  [e pc op operands]\\n    46\\t  (entity-datoms e (into [[:yin.code/segment seg] [:yin.code/pc pc]\\n    47\\t                          [:yin.code/op op]]\\n    48\\t                         (partition 2 operands))))\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t(defn- instruction\\n    52\\t  [pc op & operands]\\n    53\\t  (instruction* (eid pc) pc op operands))\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\t\\n    56\\t(defn- assemble\\n    57\\t  [& parts]\\n    58\\t  (vec (apply concat parts)))\\n    59\\t\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t(defn- make-vm\\n    62\\t  ([] (make-vm {}))\\n    63\\t  ([opts] (semantic/create-vm (merge {:make-stream tu/make-stream} opts))))\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\t\\n    66\\t(defn- ex-data-of\\n    67\\t  [thunk]\\n    68\\t  (try (thunk) nil\\n    69\\t       (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) e\\n    70\\t         (ex-data e))))\\n    71\\t\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t(defn- worked-segment\\n    74\\t  \\\"`((fn [x] (+ x 1)) 10)` as lowered in yin.vm.semantic.md S2.7.\\\"\\n    75\\t  []\\n    76\\t  (assemble (segment 14)\\n    77\\t            (instruction 0 :closure :yin.code/params '[x]\\n    78\\t                         :yin.code/body (eid 6))\\n    79\\t            (instruction 1 :push)\\n    80\\t            (instruction 2 :const :yin.code/value 10)\\n    81\\t            (instruction 3 :push)\\n    82\\t            (instruction 4 :call :yin.code/argc 1 :yin.code/tail? false)\\n    83\\t            (instruction 5 :halt)\\n    84\\t            (instruction 6 :var :yin.code/name '+)\\n    85\\t            (instruction 7 :push)\\n    86\\t            (instruction 8 :var :yin.code/name 'x)\\n    87\\t            (instruction 9 :push)\\n    88\\t            (instruction 10 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n    89\\t            (instruction 11 :push)\\n    90\\t            (instruction 12 :call :yin.code/argc 2 :yin.code/tail? true)\\n    91\\t            (instruction 13 :return)))\\n    92\\t\\n    93\\t\\n    94\\t(def ^:private worked-vector\\n    95\\t  [[:closure '[x] 6] [:push] [:const 10] [:push] [:call 1 false] [:halt]\\n    96\\t   [:var '+] [:push] [:var 'x] [:push] [:const 1] [:push] [:call 2 true]\\n    97\\t   [:return]])\\n    98\\t\\n    99\\t\\n   100\\t(defn- effects-segment\\n   101\\t  \\\"Every defaulted operand stated: a gensym, a stream-make, an ffi-call\\n   102\\t   and a non-tail call, then halt.\\\"\\n   103\\t  [& {:keys [prefix buffer argc tail?]\\n   104\\t      :or {prefix \\\"id\\\", buffer vm/default-stream-capacity, argc 0,\\n   105\\t           tail? false}}]\\n   106\\t  (assemble (segment 8)\\n   107\\t            (instruction 0 :gensym :yin.code/prefix prefix)\\n   108\\t            (instruction 1 :push)\\n   109\\t            (instruction 2 :stream-make :yin.code/buffer buffer)\\n   110\\t            (instruction 3 :push)\\n   111\\t            (instruction 4 :ffi-call :yin.code/ffi-op :op/add\\n   112\\t                         :yin.code/argc argc)\\n   113\\t            (instruction 5 :push)\\n   114\\t            (instruction 6 :call :yin.code/argc 2 :yin.code/tail? tail?)\\n   115\\t            (instruction 7 :halt)))\\n   116\\t\\n   117\\t\\n   118\\t(defn- without-attrs\\n   119\\t  \\\"The batch with every datom on one of `attrs` removed.\\\"\\n   120\\t  [batch attrs]\\n   121\\t  (vec (remove (fn [[_ a]] (contains? attrs a)) batch)))\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   125\\t;; S7.3.2 the canonical instruction vector\\n   126\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   127\\t\\n   128\\t(deftest canonical-vector-of-the-worked-segment-test\\n   129\\t  (let [v (ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector (worked-segment))]\\n   130\\t    (testing \\\"pc is the index; tuples are mnemonic plus S2.4 operands\\\"\\n   131\\t      (is (= worked-vector v))\\n   132\\t      (is (= 14 (count v))))\\n   133\\t    (testing \\\"No entity id, attribute keyword or provenance survives\\\"\\n   134\\t      (is (not-any? (fn [t] (some keyword? (rest t))) v))\\n   135\\t      (is (every? vector? v)))\\n   136\\t    (testing \\\"Refs are the loader's resolved pcs\\\"\\n   137\\t      (is (= 6 (nth (nth v 0) 2))))\\n   138\\t    (testing \\\"The direct path accepts and runs what canonicalization mints\\\"\\n   139\\t      (let [loaded (semantic/load-vector (make-vm) v)]\\n   140\\t        (is (= 11 (vm/value (vm/run loaded))))\\n   141\\t        (is (= (:code (semantic/load-image (worked-segment)))\\n   142\\t               (:code (get-in loaded [:code (:program loaded)])))\\n   143\\t            \\\"projection and direct paths yield one image (S7.3.4)\\\")))))\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\t\\n   146\\t(deftest code-identity-and-contract-stamp-test\\n   147\\t  (let [outcome (ucf/canonicalize (worked-segment))]\\n   148\\t    (testing \\\"The address is dao.jing's segment key of the vector\\\"\\n   149\\t      (is (= :yin.k/ok (:yin.k/status outcome)))\\n   150\\t      (is (= worked-vector (:yin.code/vector outcome)))\\n   151\\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key worked-vector) (:yin.code/hash outcome)))\\n   152\\t      (is (= (ucf/code-address worked-vector) (:yin.code/hash outcome)))\\n   153\\t      (is (jing/segment-address? (:yin.code/hash outcome))))\\n   154\\t    (testing \\\"The stamp names the v2 contract and the envelope version\\\"\\n   155\\t      (is (= {:yin.code/contract \\\"v2\\\", :yin.k/version 0}\\n   156\\t             (:yin.k/contract outcome)))\\n   157\\t      (is (= ucf/contract-stamp (:yin.k/contract outcome))))))\\n   158\\t\\n   159\\t\\n   160\\t(deftest loader-verifies-the-address-canonicalization-mints-test\\n   161\\t  (let [address (:yin.code/hash (ucf/canonicalize (effects-segment)))\\n   162\\t        claimed (fn [batch]\\n   163\\t                  (into (segment 8 [[:yin.code/hash address]])\\n   164\\t                        (remove (fn [[e]] (= seg e)) batch)))]\\n   165\\t    (testing \\\"A batch stating its defaults earns the claim\\\"\\n   166\\t      (is (= address (:address (semantic/load-image\\n   167\\t                                 (claimed (effects-segment)))))))\\n   168\\t    (testing \\\"A batch omitting them earns the same claim: one canonicalizer\\\"\\n   169\\t      (is (= address\\n   170\\t             (:address (semantic/load-image\\n   171\\t                         (claimed (without-attrs\\n   172\\t                                    (effects-segment)\\n   173\\t                                    #{:yin.code/prefix :yin.code/buffer\\n   174\\t                                      :yin.code/tail?})))))))\\n   175\\t    (testing \\\"A different segment does not\\\"\\n   176\\t      (is (= {:rule :hash-mismatch, :entity seg}\\n   177\\t             (:defect (ex-data-of\\n   178\\t                        #(semantic/load-image\\n   179\\t                           (claimed (effects-segment :prefix \\\"g\\\"))))))))))\\n   180\\t\\n   181\\t\\n   182\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   183\\t;; Equivalence laws\\n   184\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   185\\t\\n   186\\t(deftest equivalence-under-relabelling-and-reordering-test\\n   187\\t  (let [reference (ucf/canonicalize (worked-segment))\\n   188\\t        same? (fn [batch]\\n   189\\t                (let [o (ucf/canonicalize batch)]\\n   190\\t                  (and (= (:yin.code/vector reference) (:yin.code/vector o))\\n   191\\t                       (= (:yin.code/hash reference) (:yin.code/hash o)))))]\\n   192\\t    (testing \\\"Entity ids are relabelled: alpha-equivalent batches agree\\\"\\n   193\\t      (is (same? (mapv (fn [[e a v t m]]\\n   194\\t                         [(* 1000 e)\\n   195\\t                          a\\n   196\\t                          (if (contains? #{:yin.code/segment :yin.code/body}\\n   197\\t                                         a)\\n   198\\t                            (* 1000 v)\\n   199\\t                            v)\\n   200\\t                          t m])\\n   201\\t                       (worked-segment)))))\\n   202\\t    (testing \\\"Datom order within an entity, and the segment's place, are\\n   203\\t              transport accidents\\\"\\n   204\\t      (let [by-entity (group-by first (worked-segment))\\n   205\\t            shuffled (into []\\n   206\\t                           (mapcat (fn [e] (vec (reverse (get by-entity e)))))\\n   207\\t                           (conj (mapv eid (range 14)) seg))]\\n   208\\t        (is (same? shuffled))))\\n   209\\t    (testing \\\"Provenance is excluded\\\"\\n   210\\t      (is (same? (into (worked-segment)\\n   211\\t                       (into [[seg :yin.code/derived-from -99 0\\n   212\\t                               datom/default-op]]\\n   213\\t                             (map (fn [pc]\\n   214\\t                                    [(eid pc) :yin.code/source (- -100 pc)\\n   215\\t                                     0 datom/default-op]))\\n   216\\t                             (range 14))))))))\\n   217\\t\\n   218\\t\\n   219\\t(deftest equivalence-under-saturation-and-resolution-test\\n   220\\t  (let [stated (ucf/canonicalize (effects-segment))]\\n   221\\t    (testing \\\"Omitted defaulted operands are materialized (S7.3.2); an\\n   222\\t              argc is not omissible, S2.6 rule 7 requires it on the batch\\\"\\n   223\\t      (let [omitted (ucf/canonicalize\\n   224\\t                      (without-attrs (effects-segment)\\n   225\\t                                     #{:yin.code/prefix :yin.code/buffer\\n   226\\t                                       :yin.code/tail?}))]\\n   227\\t        (is (= [:gensym \\\"id\\\"] (nth (:yin.code/vector omitted) 0)))\\n   228\\t        (is (= [:stream-make vm/default-stream-capacity]\\n   229\\t               (nth (:yin.code/vector omitted) 2)))\\n   230\\t        (is (= [:ffi-call :op/add 0] (nth (:yin.code/vector omitted) 4)))\\n   231\\t        (is (= [:call 2 false] (nth (:yin.code/vector omitted) 6)))\\n   232\\t        (is (= (:yin.code/vector stated) (:yin.code/vector omitted)))\\n   233\\t        (is (= (:yin.code/hash stated) (:yin.code/hash omitted)))))\\n   234\\t    (testing \\\"A repeated attribute keeps its last value, as execution does\\\"\\n   235\\t      (let [twice (assemble (segment 2)\\n   236\\t                            (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   237\\t                            [[(eid 0) :yin.code/value 10 0\\n   238\\t                              datom/default-op]]\\n   239\\t                            (instruction 1 :halt))\\n   240\\t            once (assemble (segment 2)\\n   241\\t                           (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 10)\\n   242\\t                           (instruction 1 :halt))]\\n   243\\t        (is (= [[:const 10] [:halt]]\\n   244\\t               (ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector twice)))\\n   245\\t        (is (= (:yin.code/hash (ucf/canonicalize once))\\n   246\\t               (:yin.code/hash (ucf/canonicalize twice))))))))\\n   247\\t\\n   248\\t\\n   249\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   250\\t;; Distinction laws\\n   251\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   252\\t\\n   253\\t(deftest distinction-test\\n   254\\t  (let [address (fn [batch] (:yin.code/hash (ucf/canonicalize batch)))\\n   255\\t        vector-of ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector\\n   256\\t        const-program (fn [& values]\\n   257\\t                        (assemble (segment 2)\\n   258\\t                                  (instruction 0 :const)\\n   259\\t                                  (mapv (fn [v]\\n   260\\t                                          [(eid 0) :yin.code/value v 0\\n   261\\t                                           datom/default-op])\\n   262\\t                                        values)\\n   263\\t                                  (instruction 1 :halt)))]\\n   264\\t    (testing \\\"Different constants differ\\\"\\n   265\\t      (is (not= (vector-of (const-program 1)) (vector-of (const-program 2))))\\n   266\\t      (is (not= (address (const-program 1)) (address (const-program 2)))))\\n   267\\t    (testing \\\"Resolution order is identity: 1,2 and 2,1 execute differently\\\"\\n   268\\t      (is (= [[:const 2] [:halt]] (vector-of (const-program 1 2))))\\n   269\\t      (is (= [[:const 1] [:halt]] (vector-of (const-program 2 1))))\\n   270\\t      (is (not= (address (const-program 1 2)) (address (const-program 2 1)))))\\n   271\\t    (testing \\\"A tail call is not a call\\\"\\n   272\\t      (is (not= (address (effects-segment :tail? true))\\n   273\\t                (address (effects-segment :tail? false)))))\\n   274\\t    (testing \\\"A gensym prefix, a buffer, and an argc are identity\\\"\\n   275\\t      (is (not= (address (effects-segment)) (address (effects-segment\\n   276\\t                                                       :prefix \\\"g\\\"))))\\n   277\\t      (is (not= (address (effects-segment)) (address (effects-segment\\n   278\\t                                                       :buffer 1))))\\n   279\\t      (is (not= (address (effects-segment)) (address (effects-segment\\n   280\\t                                                       :argc 1)))))\\n   281\\t    (testing \\\"A different branch target is a different control graph\\\"\\n   282\\t      (let [branch (fn [target]\\n   283\\t                     (assemble (segment 5)\\n   284\\t                               (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value true)\\n   285\\t                               (instruction 1 :branch-false\\n   286\\t                                            :yin.code/target (eid target))\\n   287\\t                               (instruction 2 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   288\\t                               (instruction 3 :const :yin.code/value 2)\\n   289\\t                               (instruction 4 :halt)))]\\n   290\\t        (is (not= (address (branch 3)) (address (branch 4))))))))\\n   291\\t\\n   292\\t\\n   293\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   294\\t;; Non-canonicalizable batches\\n   295\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   296\\t\\n   297\\t(defn- refusal-of\\n   298\\t  [batch]\\n   299\\t  (ex-data-of #(ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector batch)))\\n   300\\t\\n   301\\t\\n   302\\t(deftest non-canonicalizable-test\\n   303\\t  (testing \\\"An unknown mnemonic is refused as non-portable\\\"\\n   304\\t    (let [bad (assemble (segment 2)\\n   305\\t                        (instruction 0 :frobnicate)\\n   306\\t                        (instruction 1 :halt))\\n   307\\t          r (refusal-of bad)]\\n   308\\t      (is (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status r)))\\n   309\\t      (is (= :non-canonicalizable (:yin.k/kind r)))\\n   310\\t      (is (= r (ucf/canonicalize bad))\\n   311\\t          \\\"canonicalize returns the same outcome rather than throwing\\\")))\\n   312\\t  (testing \\\"An operand the tuple has no slot for is refused, naming the pc\\\"\\n   313\\t    (let [r (refusal-of (assemble (segment 2)\\n   314\\t                                  (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1\\n   315\\t                                               :yin.code/argc 3)\\n   316\\t                                  (instruction 1 :halt)))]\\n   317\\t      (is (= :non-canonicalizable (:yin.k/kind r)))\\n   318\\t      (is (= [:pc 0] (:yin.k/path r)))\\n   319\\t      (is (= :operand-slot (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :rule])))\\n   320\\t      (is (= [:yin.code/argc] (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :attrs])))))\\n   321\\t  (testing \\\"A segment attribute beyond the folded and excluded set is refused\\\"\\n   322\\t    (let [r (refusal-of (assemble (segment 2 [[:yin.code/entry 1]])\\n   323\\t                                  (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   324\\t                                  (instruction 1 :halt)))]\\n   325\\t      (is (= :non-canonicalizable (:yin.k/kind r)))\\n   326\\t      (is (= [:entity seg] (:yin.k/path r)))\\n   327\\t      (is (= :segment-attribute (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :rule])))))\\n   328\\t  (testing \\\"An operand of the wrong kind resolves to an inadmissible tuple\\\"\\n   329\\t    (let [r (refusal-of (assemble (segment 2)\\n   330\\t                                  (instruction 0 :var :yin.code/name \\\"x\\\")\\n   331\\t                                  (instruction 1 :halt)))]\\n   332\\t      (is (= :non-canonicalizable (:yin.k/kind r)))\\n   333\\t      (is (= :operand-kind (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :rule])))\\n   334\\t      (is (= 0 (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :pc])))))\\n   335\\t  (testing \\\"A batch that is not a segment is refused with the loader's rule\\\"\\n   336\\t    (let [r (refusal-of (assemble (segment 2)\\n   337\\t                                  (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   338\\t                                  (instruction 1 :push)))]\\n   339\\t      (is (= :non-canonicalizable (:yin.k/kind r)))\\n   340\\t      (is (= :missing-terminator (get-in r [:yin.k/defect :rule])))))\\n   341\\t  (testing \\\"Nothing in the folded and excluded set is refused\\\"\\n   342\\t    (is (vector? (ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector\\n   343\\t                   (assemble (segment 2 [[:yin.code/derived-from -50]\\n   344\\t                                         [:yin.code/hash :segment/x]])\\n   345\\t                             (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   346\\t                             (instruction 1 :halt)))))))\\n   347\\t\\n   348\\t\\n   349\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   350\\t;; S7.4 safepoints: the static half\\n   351\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   352\\t\\n   353\\t(deftest transitions-cover-the-vocabulary-test\\n   354\\t  (testing \\\"Every S2.4 mnemonic is classified exactly once\\\"\\n   355\\t    (is (= #{:const :var :closure :push :call :return :jump :branch-false\\n   356\\t             :halt :gensym :store-get :store-put :stream-make :stream-put\\n   357\\t             :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close :park :resume\\n   358\\t             :current-continuation :ffi-call}\\n   359\\t           (set (keys ucf/transitions)))))\\n   360\\t  (testing \\\"The parking transitions are S7.4.1's, effectful calls included\\\"\\n   361\\t    (is (= #{:park :stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :call}\\n   362\\t           (set (keep (fn [[m k]] (when (= :parking k) m))\\n   363\\t                      ucf/transitions)))))\\n   364\\t  (testing \\\"Reifying a continuation is not a safepoint\\\"\\n   365\\t    (is (= :step (get ucf/transitions :current-continuation)))))\\n   366\\t\\n   367\\t\\n   368\\t(deftest safepoints-of-the-worked-segment-test\\n   369\\t  (let [sps (ucf/safepoints worked-vector)]\\n   370\\t    (testing \\\"The two calls are the safepoints; resume is pc+1\\\"\\n   371\\t      (is (= [4 12] (mapv :yin.safepoint/at sps)))\\n   372\\t      (is (= [5 13] (mapv :yin.safepoint/pc sps)))\\n   373\\t      (is (= [[:call-effect] [:call-effect]]\\n   374\\t             (mapv :yin.safepoint/reasons sps))))\\n   375\\t    (testing \\\"Stack effect pops operator and arguments\\\"\\n   376\\t      (is (= [-2 -3] (mapv :yin.safepoint/stack-effect sps))))\\n   377\\t    (testing \\\"Nothing is read from E after either call\\\"\\n   378\\t      (is (= [[] []] (mapv :yin.safepoint/lexically-required sps))))))\\n   379\\t\\n   380\\t\\n   381\\t(deftest stack-effect-per-parking-kind-test\\n   382\\t  (is (= 0 (ucf/stack-effect [:park])))\\n   383\\t  (is (= 0 (ucf/stack-effect [:stream-next])))\\n   384\\t  (is (= -1 (ucf/stack-effect [:stream-put])))\\n   385\\t  (is (= -2 (ucf/stack-effect [:ffi-call :op/add 2])))\\n   386\\t  (is (= -1 (ucf/stack-effect [:call 0 false])))\\n   387\\t  (is (= -3 (ucf/stack-effect [:call 2 true])))\\n   388\\t  (is (nil? (ucf/stack-effect [:const 1]))))\\n   389\\t\\n   390\\t\\n   391\\t(deftest safepoint-table-and-lookup-test\\n   392\\t  (let [v [[:stream-make 8] [:stream-cursor] [:stream-next] [:push]\\n   393\\t           [:var 'q] [:halt]]\\n   394\\t        table (ucf/safepoint-table v)]\\n   395\\t    (testing \\\"The table is keyed by the code's address and an engine profile\\\"\\n   396\\t      (is (= (jing/segment-key v) (:yin.safepoint/segment table)))\\n   397\\t      (is (= :yin.vm.semantic/reference (:yin.safepoint/engine table)))\\n   398\\t      (is (= :wasm32/v1\\n   399\\t             (:yin.safepoint/engine (ucf/safepoint-table v :wasm32/v1))))\\n   400\\t      (is (= [{:yin.safepoint/at 2,\\n   401\\t               :yin.safepoint/pc 3,\\n   402\\t               :yin.safepoint/reasons [:next],\\n   403\\t               :yin.safepoint/stack-effect 0,\\n   404\\t               :yin.safepoint/lexically-required '[q]}]\\n   405\\t             (:yin.safepoint/safepoints table))))\\n   406\\t    (testing \\\"A pc that is not a safepoint is named\\\"\\n   407\\t      (is (= 3 (:yin.safepoint/pc (ucf/safepoint-at v 3))))\\n   408\\t      (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-at-safepoint, :yin.k/pc 4}\\n   409\\t             (ucf/safepoint-at v 4))))))\\n   410\\t\\n   411\\t\\n   412\\t(deftest lexically-required-test\\n   413\\t  (let [v [[:park]\\n   414\\t           [:var 'x]\\n   415\\t           [:push]\\n   416\\t           [:closure '[y] 7]\\n   417\\t           [:push]\\n   418\\t           [:call 1 false]\\n   419\\t           [:halt]\\n   420\\t           [:var 'z]\\n   421\\t           [:push]\\n   422\\t           [:var 'y]\\n   423\\t           [:push]\\n   424\\t           [:closure '[z] 13]\\n   425\\t           [:return]\\n   426\\t           [:var 'z]\\n   427\\t           [:push]\\n   428\\t           [:var 'w]\\n   429\\t           [:return]]]\\n   430\\t    (testing \\\"Names read on every path from the resume pc; a closure body\\n   431\\t              entered from its creation has its params bound\\\"\\n   432\\t      (is (= '[w x z] (ucf/lexically-required v 1)))\\n   433\\t      (is (= '[w x z] (:yin.safepoint/lexically-required\\n   434\\t                        (first (ucf/safepoints v)))))\\n   435\\t      (is (= '[w] (ucf/lexically-required v 11))\\n   436\\t          \\\"z is bound by the inner closure created at 11\\\"))\\n   437\\t    (testing \\\"Resuming inside a body, its params are E's business: the\\n   438\\t              static walk reports them as read, the frame carries them\\\"\\n   439\\t      (is (= '[w y z] (ucf/lexically-required v 7)))\\n   440\\t      (is (= '[w z] (ucf/lexically-required v 13)))))\\n   441\\t  (testing \\\"Both arms of a branch are read; a jump skips nothing reachable\\\"\\n   442\\t    (let [v [[:const true] [:branch-false 4] [:var 'a] [:jump 5] [:var 'b]\\n   443\\t             [:halt]]]\\n   444\\t      (is (= '[a b] (ucf/lexically-required v 0)))\\n   445\\t      (is (= '[b] (ucf/lexically-required v 4)))))\\n   446\\t  (testing \\\"A loop terminates and reads each name once\\\"\\n   447\\t    (let [v [[:var 'n] [:push] [:jump 0]]]\\n   448\\t      (is (= '[n] (ucf/lexically-required v 0)))))\\n   449\\t  (testing \\\"A resume transfers control: nothing after it is reachable\\\"\\n   450\\t    (is (= '[] (ucf/lexically-required [[:resume :parked-0] [:var 'x]\\n   451\\t                                        [:halt]]\\n   452\\t                                       0)))))\\n   453\\t\\n   454\\t\\n   455\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   456\\t;; S7.4 safepoints: conformance against the reference machine\\n   457\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   458\\t\\n   459\\t(defn- step-to\\n   460\\t  \\\"Step the loaded machine until control sits at `pc`.\\\"\\n   461\\t  [vm pc]\\n   462\\t  (loop [vm vm\\n   463\\t         n 0]\\n   464\\t    (cond\\n   465\\t      (= pc (:pc (vm/control vm))) vm\\n   466\\t      (< 64 n) (throw (ex-info \\\"pc never reached\\\" {:pc pc}))\\n   467\\t      :else (recur (vm/step vm) (inc n)))))\\n   468\\t\\n   469\\t\\n   470\\t(defn- observe-park\\n   471\\t  \\\"Run the batch to the parking instruction at `at`, take the transition,\\n   472\\t   and return the operand depth before it with the frame the machine left:\\n   473\\t   a wait entry, or the parked record for an explicit park.\\\"\\n   474\\t  [vm batch at]\\n   475\\t  (let [before (step-to (semantic/vm-load-program vm batch) at)\\n   476\\t        after (vm/step before)\\n   477\\t        frame (or (first (:wait-set after))\\n   478\\t                  (first (vals (:parked after))))]\\n   479\\t    {:depth (count (:stack before)), :frame frame, :vm after}))\\n   480\\t\\n   481\\t\\n   482\\t(defn- conforms?\\n   483\\t  \\\"The static safepoint entry agrees with the observed frame: the frame's\\n   484\\t   pc is the canonical resume pc and its stack depth is the depth before\\n   485\\t   the instruction plus the static stack effect.\\\"\\n   486\\t  [batch at {:keys [depth frame]}]\\n   487\\t  (let [sp (ucf/safepoint-at (ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector batch)\\n   488\\t                             (inc at))]\\n   489\\t    (and (= at (:yin.safepoint/at sp))\\n   490\\t         (= (:yin.safepoint/pc sp) (:pc frame))\\n   491\\t         (= (+ depth (:yin.safepoint/stack-effect sp))\\n   492\\t            (count (:stack frame))))))\\n   493\\t\\n   494\\t\\n   495\\t(defn- one-slot-writer\\n   496\\t  \\\"A `:make-stream` whose handle answers `full` once one value sits in it,\\n   497\\t   so a second put parks. The reader surface is the minimum the FFI pair's\\n   498\\t   construction needs: a cursor that never advances.\\\"\\n   499\\t  [_capacity]\\n   500\\t  (let [slot (atom ::empty)]\\n   501\\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\\n   502\\t     :dao.stream/handle\\n   503\\t     (reify\\n   504\\t       stream/IDaoStreamReader\\n   505\\t       (cursor\\n   506\\t         [_ _anchor]\\n   507\\t         {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok, :dao.stream/cursor 0})\\n   508\\t\\n   509\\t       (next\\n   510\\t         [_ _cursor]\\n   511\\t         {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/blocked})\\n   512\\t\\n   513\\t\\n   514\\t       stream/IDaoStreamWriter\\n   515\\t\\n   516\\t       (append!\\n   517\\t         [_ v]\\n   518\\t         (if (= ::empty @slot)\\n   519\\t           (do (reset! slot v) {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})\\n   520\\t           {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/full})))}))\\n   521\\t\\n   522\\t\\n   523\\t(deftest explicit-park-conformance-test\\n   524\\t  (let [batch (assemble (segment 4)\\n   525\\t                        (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 5)\\n   526\\t                        (instruction 1 :push)\\n   527\\t                        (instruction 2 :park)\\n   528\\t                        (instruction 3 :halt))\\n   529\\t        obs (observe-park (make-vm) batch 2)]\\n   530\\t    (is (= 1 (:depth obs)))\\n   531\\t    (is (= 3 (:pc (:frame obs))))\\n   532\\t    (is (conforms? batch 2 obs))))\\n   533\\t\\n   534\\t\\n   535\\t(deftest blocked-read-conformance-test\\n   536\\t  (let [batch (assemble (segment 6)\\n   537\\t                        (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value :below)\\n   538\\t                        (instruction 1 :push)\\n   539\\t                        (instruction 2 :stream-make :yin.code/buffer 8)\\n   540\\t                        (instruction 3 :stream-cursor)\\n   541\\t                        (instruction 4 :stream-next)\\n   542\\t                        (instruction 5 :halt))\\n   543\\t        obs (observe-park (make-vm) batch 4)]\\n   544\\t    (is (vm/blocked? (:vm obs)))\\n   545\\t    (is (= :next (:reason (:frame obs))))\\n   546\\t    (is (= 1 (:depth obs)))\\n   547\\t    (is (conforms? batch 4 obs))))\\n   548\\t\\n   549\\t\\n   550\\t(deftest blocked-write-conformance-test\\n   551\\t  (let [batch (assemble (segment 8)\\n   552\\t                        (instruction 0 :stream-make :yin.code/buffer 1)\\n   553\\t                        (instruction 1 :push)\\n   554\\t                        (instruction 2 :push)\\n   555\\t                        (instruction 3 :const :yin.code/value :a)\\n   556\\t                        (instruction 4 :stream-put)\\n   557\\t                        (instruction 5 :const :yin.code/value :b)\\n   558\\t                        (instruction 6 :stream-put)\\n   559\\t                        (instruction 7 :halt))\\n   560\\t        obs (observe-park (make-vm {:make-stream one-slot-writer}) batch 6)]\\n   561\\t    (is (vm/blocked? (:vm obs)))\\n   562\\t    (is (= :put (:reason (:frame obs))))\\n   563\\t    (is (= 1 (:depth obs)) \\\"one target ref remains below the popped one\\\")\\n   564\\t    (is (conforms? batch 6 obs))))\\n   565\\t\\n   566\\t\\n   567\\t(deftest ffi-call-conformance-test\\n   568\\t  (let [batch (assemble (segment 6)\\n   569\\t                        (instruction 0 :const :yin.code/value 1)\\n   570\\t                        (instruction 1 :push)\\n   571\\t                        (instruction 2 :const :yin.code/value 2)\\n   572\\t                        (instruction 3 :push)\\n   573\\t                        (instruction 4 :ffi-call :yin.code/ffi-op :op/add\\n   574\\t                                     :yin.code/argc 1)\\n   575\\t                        (instruction 5 :halt))\\n   576\\t        obs (observe-park (make-vm) batch 4)]\\n   577\\t    (is (vm/blocked? (:vm obs)))\\n   578\\t    (is (= 2 (:depth obs)))\\n   579\\t    (is (= [1] (:stack (:frame obs))) \\\"the argument is popped, not the 1\\\")\\n   580\\t    (is (conforms? batch 4 obs))))\\n   581\\t\\n   582\\t\\n   583\\t(deftest effectful-call-conformance-test\\n   584\\t  (let [blocking-next (fn [cursor] {:effect :stream/next, :cursor cursor})\\n   585\\t        batch (assemble (segment 7)\\n   586\\t                        (instruction 0 :var :yin.code/name 'blocking-next)\\n   587\\t                        (instruction 1 :push)\\n   588\\t                        (instruction 2 :stream-make :yin.code/buffer 8)\\n   589\\t                        (instruction 3 :stream-cursor)\\n   590\\t                        (instruction 4 :push)\\n   591\\t                        (instruction 5 :call :yin.code/argc 1\\n   592\\t                                     :yin.code/tail? false)\\n   593\\t                        (instruction 6 :halt))\\n   594\\t        obs (observe-park (make-vm {:primitives {'blocking-next blocking-next}})\\n   595\\t                          batch 5)]\\n   596\\t    (is (vm/blocked? (:vm obs)))\\n   597\\t    (is (= :next (:reason (:frame obs))))\\n   598\\t    (is (= 2 (:depth obs)) \\\"operator and cursor were on the stack\\\")\\n   599\\t    (is (= [] (:stack (:frame obs))))\\n   600\\t    (is (conforms? batch 5 obs))))\\n   601\\t\\n   602\\t\\n   603\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   604\\t;; The dynamic half rides in the frame\\n   605\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   606\\t\\n   607\\t(deftest activation-state-and-requirements-test\\n   608\\t  (let [v [[:stream-make 8] [:stream-cursor] [:stream-next] [:push]\\n   609\\t           [:var 'q] [:push] [:var '+] [:halt]]\\n   610\\t        sp (ucf/safepoint-at v 3)\\n   611\\t        frame {:segment seg, :pc 3, :env {'q 1, 'r 2},\\n   612\\t               :stack [:x :y], :k [{:type :return, :stack-base 1}]}]\\n   613\\t    (testing \\\"Static: what the code will read\\\"\\n   614\\t      (is (= '[+ q] (:yin.safepoint/lexically-required sp))))\\n   615\\t    (testing \\\"Dynamic: depth, bound names and bases come from the frame\\\"\\n   616\\t      (is (= {:yin.k/depth 2, :yin.k/bound '[q r], :yin.k/stack-bases [1]}\\n   617\\t             (ucf/activation-state frame))))\\n   618\\t    (testing \\\"A requirement is met by E or by the rest of resolution\\\"\\n   619\\t      (is (= '[+] (ucf/unsatisfied-names sp frame (constantly false))))\\n   620\\t      (is (= '[] (ucf/unsatisfied-names sp frame #{'+})))\\n   621\\t      (is (= '[+ q]\\n   622\\t             (ucf/unsatisfied-names sp {:env {}} (constantly false)))))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:27:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '95,520p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   vector, naming the offending place (`:yin.k/path`) and the defect.\\\"\\n  [path defect hint]\\n  {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable,\\n   :yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable,\\n   :yin.k/path path,\\n   :yin.k/defect defect,\\n   :yin.k/hint hint})\\n\\n\\n(defn- refuse\\n  [path defect hint]\\n  (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Cannot canonicalize segment: \\\" hint)\\n                  (refusal path defect hint))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- resolved-batch\\n  \\\"The loader's reading of a batch: `{:seg :attrs :instructions}` with\\n   instructions in pc order. A batch that is not well formed (S2.6) is not\\n   a segment, and is refused with the loader's own defect.\\\"\\n  [datoms]\\n  (when-let [defect (code/well-formed? datoms)]\\n    (refuse [:entity (:entity defect)]\\n            defect\\n            (str \\\"the batch is not well formed: \\\" (name (:rule defect)))))\\n  (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\\n        seg (some #(when (= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type])) %)\\n                  order)\\n        instructions (filterv (fn [e]\\n                                (and (not= seg e)\\n                                     (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\\n                                           structural-attrs)))\\n                              order)]\\n    {:seg seg, :attrs attrs, :instructions instructions}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- check-segment-entity\\n  [seg attrs]\\n  (let [extra (remove segment-attrs (keys (get attrs seg)))]\\n    (when (seq extra)\\n      (refuse [:entity seg]\\n              {:rule :segment-attribute, :entity seg, :attrs (vec extra)}\\n              \\\"the segment entity carries an attribute with no slot\\\"))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- canonical-tuple\\n  \\\"One instruction's tuple: the mnemonic, then its S2.4 operands in table\\n   order, refs resolved to pcs and defaults materialized. Refused when the\\n   mnemonic has no tuple or the instruction carries an operand its tuple\\n   has no slot for.\\\"\\n  [pc-of pc e ia]\\n  (let [mnem (:yin.code/op ia)\\n        operands (get code/vector-operand-table mnem)]\\n    (when-not operands\\n      (refuse [:pc pc]\\n              {:rule :mnemonic, :pc pc, :entity e, :op mnem}\\n              \\\"the mnemonic is outside the S2.4 table\\\"))\\n    (let [slots (into #{} (map first) operands)\\n          extra (remove (fn [a]\\n                          (or (structural-attrs a)\\n                              (excluded-instruction-attrs a)\\n                              (slots a)))\\n                        (keys ia))]\\n      (when (seq extra)\\n        (refuse [:pc pc]\\n                {:rule :operand-slot, :pc pc, :entity e, :attrs (vec extra)}\\n                \\\"the instruction carries an operand its tuple has no slot for\\\"))\\n      (into [mnem]\\n            (map (fn [[a kind]]\\n                   (let [v (get ia a)]\\n                     (cond\\n                       (= :pc kind)\\n                       (or (get pc-of v)\\n                           (refuse [:pc pc]\\n                                   {:rule :dangling-target, :pc pc,\\n                                    :entity e, :ref v}\\n                                   \\\"a ref resolves to no instruction\\\"))\\n                       (nil? v) (get saturation-defaults [mnem a])\\n                       :else v))))\\n            operands))))\\n\\n\\n(defn batch->canonical-instruction-vector\\n  \\\"The canonical instruction vector (S7.3.2) of one `:yin.code/*` segment\\n   batch, or a thrown `:yin.k/non-portable` refusal (`refusal`, kind\\n   `:non-canonicalizable`) when the batch has none.\\n\\n   The batch is read through the loader's resolved interpretation, so two\\n   batches that execute alike canonicalize alike: entity ids, datom order\\n   within an entity, provenance, and omitted defaulted operands leave the\\n   vector unchanged; a repeated attribute keeps its last value. The result\\n   is checked against the S7.5 tuple grammar before it is returned, so a\\n   vector this function yields is always one `load-vector` accepts.\\\"\\n  [datoms]\\n  (let [{:keys [seg attrs instructions]} (resolved-batch datoms)\\n        pc-of (zipmap instructions (range))]\\n    (check-segment-entity seg attrs)\\n    (let [v (into []\\n                  (map-indexed (fn [pc e]\\n                                 (canonical-tuple pc-of pc e (get attrs e))))\\n                  instructions)]\\n      (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\\n        (refuse [:pc (:pc defect)]\\n                defect\\n                (str \\\"the resolved tuple is not admissible: \\\"\\n                     (name (:rule defect)))))\\n      v)))\\n\\n\\n(defn code-address\\n  \\\"`:yin.code/hash` of a canonical vector: its `dao.jing` segment address.\\n   UCF mints no second addressing scheme.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (jing/segment-key v))\\n\\n\\n(defn canonicalize\\n  \\\"Code identity as one S7.9 outcome. On success\\n   `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/ok, :yin.code/vector v, :yin.code/hash address,\\n   :yin.k/contract contract-stamp}`; otherwise the `refusal` map the\\n   vector function would have thrown.\\\"\\n  [datoms]\\n  (try\\n    (let [v (batch->canonical-instruction-vector datoms)]\\n      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/ok,\\n       :yin.code/vector v,\\n       :yin.code/hash (code-address v),\\n       :yin.k/contract contract-stamp})\\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n      (let [data (ex-data e)]\\n        (if (= :yin.k/non-portable (:yin.k/status data))\\n          data\\n          (throw e))))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Safepoints (S7.4)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def transitions\\n  \\\"Every S2.4 mnemonic classified by what its S4.2 transition does to\\n   control. `:step` continues at pc+1; `:jump`, `:branch` and `:transfer`\\n   move control (a `:resume` restores a parked configuration, so its\\n   successor is not pc+1); `:terminal` leaves no successor; `:parking` may\\n   leave the machine parked with a canonical configuration at pc+1 -- the\\n   safepoints of S7.4.1. `:call` is parking because the operator may be a\\n   primitive whose result is a blocking effect; `:current-continuation`\\n   is a step, not a safepoint (it reifies and continues).\\\"\\n  {:const :step,\\n   :var :step,\\n   :closure :step,\\n   :push :step,\\n   :gensym :step,\\n   :store-get :step,\\n   :store-put :step,\\n   :stream-make :step,\\n   :stream-cursor :step,\\n   :stream-close :step,\\n   :current-continuation :step,\\n   :jump :jump,\\n   :branch-false :branch,\\n   :resume :transfer,\\n   :return :terminal,\\n   :halt :terminal,\\n   :park :parking,\\n   :stream-next :parking,\\n   :stream-put :parking,\\n   :ffi-call :parking,\\n   :call :parking})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private parking-reasons\\n  \\\"S7.4.1's safepoint kinds, by parking mnemonic. An `:ffi-call` parks\\n   under two reasons -- sent, or retained while the request is in hand --\\n   at one safepoint.\\\"\\n  {:park [:park],\\n   :stream-next [:next],\\n   :stream-put [:put],\\n   :ffi-call [:ffi :ffi-request],\\n   :call [:call-effect]})\\n\\n\\n(defn stack-effect\\n  \\\"Delta |St| across one parking instruction, from the tuple alone\\n   (S7.4.1's `Stack in frame` column): a park and a blocked read leave St;\\n   a blocked write has popped its target; an FFI call its arguments; an\\n   effectful call its operator and arguments.\\\"\\n  [t]\\n  (case (nth t 0)\\n    :park 0\\n    :stream-next 0\\n    :stream-put -1\\n    :ffi-call (- (nth t 2))\\n    :call (- (inc (nth t 1)))\\n    nil))\\n\\n\\n(defn- successors\\n  \\\"The pcs control may reach next from `pc`, with the names bound on the\\n   way: a closure's body is entered with its params bound, everything else\\n   keeps the current binding set.\\\"\\n  [v pc bound]\\n  (let [t (nth v pc)\\n        mnem (nth t 0)]\\n    (case (get transitions mnem)\\n      :step (if (= :closure mnem)\\n              [[(inc pc) bound] [(nth t 2) (into bound (nth t 1))]]\\n              [[(inc pc) bound]])\\n      :parking [[(inc pc) bound]]\\n      :jump [[(nth t 1) bound]]\\n      :branch [[(inc pc) bound] [(nth t 1) bound]]\\n      [])))\\n\\n\\n(defn lexically-required\\n  \\\"The names the code reachable from `pc` reads from E, from the vector\\n   alone (S7.4.2): every `:var` name on a control path from `pc` --\\n   through fall-through, jumps, branches, and the bodies of closures\\n   created on the way -- minus the names those closures bind. Callees are\\n   values, not code reachable here: a closure already on the stack carries\\n   its own captured environment, which is the frame's business. Sorted by\\n   name so the result is a stable value.\\\"\\n  [v pc]\\n  (loop [work [[pc #{}]]\\n         seen #{}\\n         names #{}]\\n    (if-let [[p bound :as item] (peek work)]\\n      (let [work (pop work)]\\n        (if (contains? seen item)\\n          (recur work seen names)\\n          (let [t (nth v p)\\n                names (if (and (= :var (nth t 0))\\n                               (not (contains? bound (nth t 1))))\\n                        (conj names (nth t 1))\\n                        names)]\\n            (recur (into work (successors v p bound))\\n                   (conj seen item)\\n                   names))))\\n      (vec (sort-by str names)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn safepoints\\n  \\\"The static safepoint map of one canonical vector (S7.4.2): one entry per\\n   parking instruction, each carrying only what the vector alone fixes --\\n   the parking pc, the canonical resume pc (pc+1, always inside the segment\\n   by S2.6), the reasons under which the machine parks there, the stack\\n   effect across the instruction, and the lexically required names at the\\n   resume pc. Absolute depth, activation bases, captured environments and\\n   physical layouts are not here: they ride in the frame\\n   (`activation-state`).\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (into []\\n        (keep-indexed\\n          (fn [pc t]\\n            (when (= :parking (get transitions (nth t 0)))\\n              {:yin.safepoint/at pc,\\n               :yin.safepoint/pc (inc pc),\\n               :yin.safepoint/reasons (get parking-reasons (nth t 0)),\\n               :yin.safepoint/stack-effect (stack-effect t),\\n               :yin.safepoint/lexically-required\\n               (lexically-required v (inc pc))})))\\n        v))\\n\\n\\n(defn safepoint-table\\n  \\\"`safepoints` published per segment, keyed by the code's address and the\\n   engine profile that derived it (the reference machine by default).\\\"\\n  ([v] (safepoint-table v :yin.vm.semantic/reference))\\n  ([v engine]\\n   {:yin.safepoint/segment (code-address v),\\n    :yin.safepoint/engine engine,\\n    :yin.safepoint/safepoints (safepoints v)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn safepoint-at\\n  \\\"The static safepoint entry whose canonical resume pc is `pc`, or the\\n   S7.9 `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` outcome naming the pc.\\\"\\n  [v pc]\\n  (or (some #(when (= pc (:yin.safepoint/pc %)) %) (safepoints v))\\n      {:yin.k/status :yin.k/not-at-safepoint, :yin.k/pc pc}))\\n\\n\\n(defn activation-state\\n  \\\"The dynamic half of a parked configuration, read off a frame -- a wait\\n   entry or parked record `{segment pc env stack k}` -- never derived from\\n   code: the absolute operand-stack depth, the names E actually binds, and\\n   each return frame's activation base.\\\"\\n  [frame]\\n  {:yin.k/depth (count (:stack frame)),\\n   :yin.k/bound (vec (sort-by str (keys (:env frame)))),\\n   :yin.k/stack-bases (mapv :stack-base (:k frame))})\\n\\n\\n(defn unsatisfied-names\\n  \\\"The lexically required names of a static safepoint that the frame's\\n   environment does not bind and that `resolvable` (store, primitives,\\n   modules -- the rest of `resolve-var`'s order) does not answer: the\\n   conformance check that a frame carries what its code will read.\\\"\\n  [safepoint frame resolvable]\\n  (vec (remove (fn [n]\\n                 (or (contains? (:env frame) n) (resolvable n)))\\n               (:yin.safepoint/lexically-required safepoint))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:38:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '50,105p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '130,285p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; sed -n '720,750p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\\nvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\\nresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\\nserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\\nstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\\nsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\\n\\n### 1.2 The six invariants\\n\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\\n|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\\n|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\\n|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\\n|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\\n|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\\n|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\\n|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\\n|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\\n|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\\n|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\\n|                          | changes.                                                                         |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\\n|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\\n|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n---\\n\\n## §2. The linear executable datom specification\\n\\n### 2.1 Tuple format\\n\\nExecutable datoms are canonical 5-tuples `[e a v t m]`, produced the same\\nway `yin.vm/ast->datoms` produces AST datoms: negative tempids for `e`,\\n`t` = 0 unless the linearizer is given one, `m` = `dao.datom/default-op`.\\nAttributes live under the `:yin.code/*` namespace so that\\n`engine/executable-program-datom?` (which selects `:yin/*`) does not confuse\\na code segment with an AST program, and a query can select either view.\\n\\n### 2.2 The segment\\n\\nA segment is one entity plus its instructions. It is the unit a program\\nstream carries (one batch value), the unit the loader accepts, and the unit\\na closure or continuation names.\\n| Attribute           | Value                    | Required on                                           |\\n+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/segment` | ref (segment eid)        | every instruction                                     |\\n+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/pc`      | int                      | every instruction                                     |\\n+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/op`      | keyword mnemonic (below) | every instruction                                     |\\n+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/source`  | ref (AST node eid)       | every lowered instruction; absent when hand-assembled |\\n+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nLinearity is therefore an **explicit fact**, `:yin.code/pc`, not the vector\\norder of the batch and not entity-id arithmetic. Entity ids are tempids that\\na transactor may remap; vector order is a transport accident. The\\nwell-formedness rule (§2.6) requires the batch to be *sorted* by pc so a\\nloader can stream it, but the pc fact is the truth.\\n\\nBranch and closure targets are **refs to instruction entities**\\n(`:yin.code/target`, `:yin.code/body`), not pc integers. The loader resolves\\neach ref to a pc through the index it builds. This keeps the datom semantic\\n(\\\"this branch goes to that instruction\\\"), survives id remapping when both\\nends are remapped together, and is checkable.\\n\\n### 2.4 The core semantic primitives\\n\\nMachine model: an **accumulator machine with an operand stack** (§4). `val`\\nis the accumulator; `St` the operand stack; `K` the continuation of return\\nframes. This is the stack-class formulation `streams-all-the-way-down.md` §3\\nsays survives the log-structured reading; a register model would not.\\n\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/op`          | Operand attributes                            | Effect on the machine                                                            |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:const`                | `:yin.code/value v`                           | `val ← v`                                                                        |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:var`                  | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, sym)`                                       |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:call`                 | `:yin.code/argc n`, `:yin.code/tail? bool`    | pop `n` args and the operator below them; apply (§4.2)                           |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:return`               | —                                             | pop frame from `K`; restore `pc`, `E`, stack base; `val` unchanged; empty `K`    |\\n|                         |                                               | halts                                                                            |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:jump`                 | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← target`                                                                    |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:branch-false`         | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← val ? pc+1 : target`                                                       |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:halt`                 | —                                             | halt with `val` as the result                                                    |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:gensym`               | `:yin.code/prefix`                            | `val ← fresh id`; id counter advances                                            |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:store-get`            | `:yin.code/key`                               | `val ← S[key]`                                                                   |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:store-put`            | `:yin.code/key`, `:yin.code/value`            | `S[key] ← v; val ← v`                                                            |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:stream-make`          | `:yin.code/buffer`                            | effect `:stream/make` via engine                                                 |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:stream-put`           | —                                             | target ref popped from `St`, value in `val`; effect `:stream/put`                |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:stream-cursor`        | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/cursor`                                     |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:stream-next`          | —                                             | cursor ref in `val`; effect `:stream/next`                                       |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:stream-close`         | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/close`                                      |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:park`                 | —                                             | `engine/park-continuation` with the current frame                                |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:resume`               | `:yin.code/parked-id`                         | resume value in `val`; `engine/resume-continuation`                              |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:current-continuation` | —                                             | `val ← {:type :reified-continuation :segment :pc :env :stack :k}`                |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:ffi-call`             | `:yin.code/ffi-op kw`, `:yin.code/argc n`     | pop `n` args; park-and-call over the FFI pair                                    |\\n+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nThis is the AST vocabulary of `yin.vm/ast->datoms` made linear, one for\\none, plus the three sequencing primitives (`:push`, `:jump`, `:branch-false`)\\nand `:halt`. `:yin/macro-expand` and `:vm/store-update` are outside both\\nevaluators' supported corpus and are rejected at lowering with an error\\nnaming the node.\\n\\n**Opcode integers.** `yin.vm/opcode-table` already assigns integers to\\n`:literal :load-var :lambda :call :return :branch :jump :gensym :store-get\\n:store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close\\n:park :resume :current-cont :tailcall :dao.stream.apply/call`. The loader\\nmaps mnemonics onto that table (`:const`→`:literal`, `:var`→`:load-var`,\\n`:closure`→`:lambda`, `:branch-false`→`:branch`, `:current-continuation`→\\n`:current-cont`, `:ffi-call`→`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:call`+`:tail? true`→\\n`:tailcall`) and the table gains `:push 22` and `:halt 23`. `:move` stays\\nunused. Keeping the datom mnemonics semantic and the image integers\\nmechanical is the point: a query asks for `:call`, the dispatch switches on\\n`5` or `20`.\\n\\n**Saturation and defaults.** Two operand attributes fall back to a default\\nwhen absent rather than failing to decode: `:gensym`'s `:yin.code/prefix`\\ndefaults to `\\\"id\\\"`; `:stream-make`'s `:yin.code/buffer` defaults to\\n`yin.vm/default-stream-capacity`. Every other operand attribute in the\\n§2.4 table is required by its op — the decoder does not guess at it.\\n\\n**The `\\\"v2\\\"` execution contract, published.** `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`\\n§7.11 names a contract-revision-publication blocker for `:yin.code/contract\\n\\\"v2\\\"`: a revision history naming the mnemonic set, per-mnemonic arity and\\noperand kinds, the saturation/defaults table, the opcode table and\\ntransitions, resolution and last-value-wins rules, the effect outcome map,\\nand scheduler semantics, all in this section. All seven are already written,\\nhere or by direct citation:\\n\\n- **Mnemonic set**: the `:yin.code/op` column of §2.4's table, 21 values,\\n  matching `yin.vm.code/mnemonics`.\\n- **Per-mnemonic arity and operand kinds**: §2.4's \\\"Operand attributes\\\"\\n  column; enforced by §2.6 rule 2 (`:instruction-shape`) and rule 5\\n  (`:dangling-target`, for the three ref-bearing ops).\\n- **Saturation/defaults**: the paragraph directly above.\\n- **Opcode table and transitions**: the \\\"Opcode integers\\\" paragraph above\\n  for the mnemonic→integer mapping; §4.2 for the transition equations.\\n- **Resolution rule**: §4.2's $\\\\rho$ = `engine/resolve-var`,\\n  env → store → primitives → module registry (`ast.md` Part 2 gives the\\n  same order for the walker — one resolution rule, two evaluators).\\n- **Last-value-wins rule**: a repeated single-valued instruction attribute\\n  keeps its last value in datom order, exactly as `code.cljc`'s\\n  `index-batch` and `yin.vm/index-datoms` both implement it.\\n- **Effect outcome map**: §3.3's table.\\n- **Scheduler semantics**: §3.5.\\n\\nThis is the whole `\\\"v2\\\"` contract by reference, not a duplicate of it —\\neach piece has exactly one home, and this note is that home's index.\\n\\n### 2.5 Constants and literals\\n\\nA literal lives inline in the `v` slot of `:yin.code/value`, polymorphic\\nexactly as `:yin/value` is today (numbers, strings, keywords, vectors, maps,\\nsets, nil). Host functions never appear in code datoms; a primitive is named\\nby symbol in `:var` and resolved at run time through the supplied\\n`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\\nthe program. A shared or large constant pool (`:yin.code/const-ref` to a\\nconstant entity) is reserved and not needed for the current corpus.\\n\\n### 2.6 Well-formedness (checked by the loader, tested in Phase 0)\\n\\n1. Exactly one entity with `:yin.code/type :segment` per batch.\\n2. Every instruction entity names this segment (`:yin.code/segment`) and\\n   carries a §2.4 mnemonic (`:yin.code/op`); the later rules read both, so a\\n   batch that lacks them cannot be judged by them (`:instruction-shape`).\\n3. Instruction pcs are exactly `0 .. length-1`, each once.\\n4. The batch is sorted by pc (so a loader can fill the array in one pass).\\n5. Every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` resolves to an instruction\\n   of the same segment, and a `:jump`, `:branch-false`, or `:closure`\\n   without its required ref resolves nowhere and fails the same rule.\\n6. Every basic block ends in a terminator (`:jump`, `:return`, `:halt`) or\\n   falls into a labelled successor; pc `length-1` is a terminator.\\n7. Every `:call`/`:ffi-call` has a non-negative `:yin.code/argc`.\\n\\nViolation is a load error naming the entity and rule. The loader is total\\nover the outcomes of its inputs; it does not guess. `yin.vm.code/rules`\\n- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\\n  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\\n- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\\n  only at park points (\\\"immutability observable only at park points\\\",\\n  streams note §3).\\n- **Content-addressed segments** (`:yin.code/hash`) for dedup across VMs and\\n  for verifying a shipped continuation against its code.\\n\\nPhase 4 measured it: see §8.\\n\\n## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\\n\\n> [!NOTE]  \\n> The Universal Continuation Format has been extracted to its own dedicated design document as a distributed systems protocol specification. \\n> See [`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md) for the full protocol design, content-addressing mechanisms, and heterogeneous network migration invariants.\\n\\n\\n## §8. Phase 4 Benchmarks\\n\\nMeasured 2026-09-14 on the development machine (macOS, OpenJDK 21.0.2).\\n\\n**Workload.** The historic tail-recursive countdown from the deleted\\n`yin.vm.bytecode-bench`, unchanged, so the numbers read against the table in\\n`docs/cesk-space-optimization.md`. **Timed region.** `vm/run` alone: VM\\nconstruction, AST → datoms, lowering, and the program load are outside it,\\nand every timed call runs the same loaded VM value. **Harness.**\\n`test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc` (Criterium `quick-bench`; it checks the\\nresult is 0 before timing).\\n\\n### 8.1 JVM\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '520,760p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   `yin.vm.code` reads one.\\\"\\n  [datoms]\\n  (reduce (fn [[order attrs] [e a v]]\\n            [(if (contains? attrs e) order (conj order e))\\n             (assoc-in attrs [e a] v)])\\n          [[] {}]\\n          datoms))\\n\\n\\n(defn load-image\\n  \\\"Decode one `:yin.code/*` batch into an executable image.\\n\\n   Returns `{:segment id, :length n, :code instructions}` where `instructions`\\n   is a vector indexed by pc of decoded `[opcode & operands]` vectors with\\n   every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` ref resolved to a pc integer\\n   of the same segment, plus `:address` when the segment entity carries\\n   `:yin.code/hash` (§2.2) — a claim checked here: the canonical vector the\\n   batch reconstructs to must hash to it (UCF §7.3.4's content-integrity\\n   check, its `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` outcome), so a false claim never\\n   reaches the alias column. Throws a load error naming the entity and rule\\n   when the batch is not well formed (§2.6); the loader is total over the\\n   outcomes of its inputs and does not guess.\\\"\\n  [datoms]\\n  (let [defect (code/well-formed? datoms)]\\n    (when defect\\n      (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Cannot load segment: \\\" (name (:rule defect))\\n                           \\\" (entity \\\" (:entity defect) \\\")\\\")\\n                      {:defect defect})))\\n    (let [[order attrs] (index-batch datoms)\\n          segments (filterv #(= :segment (get-in attrs [% :yin.code/type]))\\n                            order)\\n          seg (first segments)\\n          structural [:yin.code/segment :yin.code/pc :yin.code/op]\\n          instructions (filterv (fn [e]\\n                                  (and (not= seg e)\\n                                       (some #(contains? (get attrs e) %)\\n                                             structural)))\\n                                order)\\n          ;; Rule 3 sorts by pc, so an instruction's position is its pc.\\n          pc-of (zipmap instructions (range))\\n          resolve-ref (fn [e ref]\\n                        (or (get pc-of ref)\\n                            (throw (ex-info\\n                                     \\\"Ref resolves to no instruction of this segment\\\"\\n                                     {:entity e, :ref ref}))))\\n          decode (fn [e]\\n                   (let [ia (get attrs e)\\n                         mnem (:yin.code/op ia)\\n                         opcode (or (get vm/opcode-table\\n                                         (get mnemonic-aliases mnem mnem))\\n                                    (throw (ex-info \\\"Mnemonic has no opcode\\\"\\n                                                    {:op mnem, :entity e})))]\\n                     (case mnem\\n                       :const [opcode (:yin.code/value ia)]\\n                       :var [opcode (:yin.code/name ia)]\\n                       :closure [opcode (:yin.code/params ia)\\n                                 (resolve-ref e (:yin.code/body ia))]\\n                       :call (if (:yin.code/tail? ia)\\n                               [(:tailcall vm/opcode-table) (:yin.code/argc ia)]\\n                               [opcode (:yin.code/argc ia)])\\n                       :return [opcode]\\n                       :jump [opcode (resolve-ref e (:yin.code/target ia))]\\n                       :branch-false [opcode\\n                                      (resolve-ref e (:yin.code/target ia))]\\n                       :halt [opcode]\\n                       :gensym [opcode (or (:yin.code/prefix ia) \\\"id\\\")]\\n                       :store-get [opcode (:yin.code/key ia)]\\n                       :store-put [opcode (:yin.code/key ia)\\n                                   (:yin.code/value ia)]\\n                       :stream-make [opcode (or (:yin.code/buffer ia)\\n                                                vm/default-stream-capacity)]\\n                       :stream-put [opcode]\\n                       :stream-cursor [opcode]\\n                       :stream-next [opcode]\\n                       :stream-close [opcode]\\n                       :park [opcode]\\n                       :resume [opcode (:yin.code/parked-id ia)]\\n                       :current-continuation [opcode]\\n                       :ffi-call [opcode (:yin.code/ffi-op ia)\\n                                  (or (:yin.code/argc ia) 0)]\\n                       :push [opcode]\\n                       (throw (ex-info \\\"Unknown mnemonic\\\"\\n                                       {:op mnem, :entity e})))))\\n          claimed (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/hash])\\n          ;; A claimed address is earned, never trusted: the batch must\\n          ;; reconstruct to the canonical vector that hashes to it.\\n          ;; UCF §7.3.4 checks an address whenever one is claimed, so a\\n          ;; false claim fails the load here and never reaches the alias\\n          ;; column. The vector is the one `yin.vm.ucf` mints, saturated,\\n          ;; so a batch omitting a defaulted operand earns the same\\n          ;; address as one stating it.\\n          canonical-vec (when claimed\\n                          (ucf/batch->canonical-instruction-vector datoms))]\\n      (if (and claimed (not (jing/segment-matches? claimed canonical-vec)))\\n        (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Cannot load segment: hash-mismatch (entity \\\"\\n                             seg \\\")\\\")\\n                        {:defect {:rule :hash-mismatch, :entity seg},\\n                         :claimed claimed,\\n                         :actual (let [algo (try\\n                                              (jing/segment-algorithm claimed)\\n                                              (catch #?(:cljd Object\\n                                                        :clj Throwable\\n                                                        :cljs :default)\\n                                                     _\\n                                                jing/default-hash-algorithm))]\\n                                   (jing/segment-key canonical-vec\\n                                                     {:algorithm algo}))}))\\n        (cond-> {:segment seg,\\n                 :length (get-in attrs [seg :yin.code/length]),\\n                 :code (mapv decode instructions)}\\n          ;; A batch carrying no hash — every `lower` output — records no\\n          ;; alias.\\n          claimed (assoc :address claimed))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- store-image\\n  \\\"Register one decoded image under its segment id, returning the code map.\\n\\n   Segment identity is stable: continuations, frames, and closures name\\n   segments by id, so an id must never come to mean different code. An\\n   identical reload — a batch re-sent, a batch run twice — decodes to the\\n   same image and is accepted. A different image under a live id is a\\n   conflict, and loading it fails rather than silently redirecting every\\n   continuation that names the id.\\\"\\n  [code image]\\n  (let [seg (:segment image)]\\n    (if-let [existing (find code seg)]\\n      (if (= (val existing) image)\\n        code\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"Cannot load segment: the id already holds different code\\\"\\n                        {:segment seg})))\\n      (assoc code seg image))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- store-alias\\n  \\\"Record one loaded image's address in the `address → local-id` alias\\n   column (UCF §7.3.4). The column is checked: an address may never land\\n   under a second live local id. An image claiming no address — every batch\\n   the datom lane's `lower` produces — leaves the column unchanged.\\\"\\n  [aliases image]\\n  (if-let [address (:address image)]\\n    (if-let [prior (find aliases address)]\\n      (if (= (val prior) (:segment image))\\n        aliases\\n        (throw (ex-info \\\"Cannot load segment: the address is already aliased to another id\\\"\\n                        {:address address, :aliased-to (val prior)})))\\n      (assoc aliases address (:segment image)))\\n    aliases))\\n\\n\\n(defn vm-load-program\\n  \\\"Load one `:yin.code/*` batch: validate it, decode it, store the image under\\n   `:code {segment-id image}`, record the segment's claimed address — verified\\n   against the batch's own content by `load-image` — in the alias column when\\n   it carries one, and set control to `{:segment id :pc 0}`.\\n\\n   This is the loader a composition hands to `dao.stream.observer/\\n   run-on-stream` beside `engine/ready-for-ingress?` and the VM's runner; a\\n   composition whose program stream carries `:yin/*` AST datoms composes the\\n   Phase 2 linearizer in front of it. Parked continuations survive a load, so\\n   one segment can resume another's. A segment id already holding different\\n   code is a load error; loading the same image again is accepted.\\\"\\n  [vm datoms]\\n  (let [image (load-image datoms)]\\n    (assoc vm\\n           :program (:segment image)\\n           :code (store-image (:code vm) image)\\n           :code-aliases (store-alias (:code-aliases vm) image)\\n           :control {:segment (:segment image), :pc 0}\\n           :stack []\\n           :k nil\\n           :halted? false\\n           :blocked? false\\n           :value nil)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- decode-tuple\\n  \\\"One canonical tuple onto the image, reading `(nth tuple i)` for every\\n   operand: mnemonics onto `vm/opcode-table` and `[:call argc tail?]`\\n   folded to `:tailcall` by the loader's rule — the fold UCF §7.3.2 leaves\\n   to the decoder. The vector is saturated and its refs are resolved pcs,\\n   so nothing defaults and nothing resolves here.\\\"\\n  [t]\\n  (if (= :call (nth t 0))\\n    (if (nth t 2)\\n      [(:tailcall vm/opcode-table) (nth t 1)]\\n      [(:call vm/opcode-table) (nth t 1)])\\n    (let [mnem (nth t 0)\\n          opcode (or (get vm/opcode-table (get mnemonic-aliases mnem mnem))\\n                     (throw (ex-info \\\"Mnemonic has no opcode\\\" {:op mnem})))]\\n      (into [opcode] (rest t)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn load-vector\\n  \\\"Load one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) by the direct path\\n   (§7.1): validate it against §7.5 — a defect throws naming the pc — then\\n   decode the positional operands into the same image `load-image` builds\\n   from the projected batch, and register it under `:code` with the\\n   vector's address `(jing/segment-key v)` in the alias column.\\n\\n   A vector claims no local id, so one is minted below the loaded floor\\n   (`vm/loaded-code-floor`), as `ast-loader` mints for lowered AST batches;\\n   an address already aliased reloads under its id, which `store-image`\\n   accepts as the identical image. Options:\\n     :id claim this local segment id instead of minting one. The §3.1 rule\\n          applies: an id already holding a different image is a load error,\\n          an identical reload is accepted. Ignored when the address is\\n          already aliased.\\\"\\n  ([vm v] (load-vector vm v {}))\\n  ([vm v opts]\\n   (when-let [defect (code/well-formed-vector? v)]\\n     (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Cannot load vector: \\\" (name (:rule defect))\\n                          \\\" (pc \\\" (:pc defect) \\\")\\\")\\n                     {:defect defect})))\\n   (let [address (jing/segment-key v)\\n         aliases (:code-aliases vm)\\n         seg (or (get aliases address)\\n                 (:id opts)\\n                 (dec (vm/loaded-code-floor (:code vm))))\\n         image {:segment seg,\\n                :length (count v),\\n                :code (mapv decode-tuple v),\\n                :address address}]\\n     (assoc vm\\n            :program seg\\n            :code (store-image (:code vm) image)\\n            :code-aliases (store-alias aliases image)\\n            :control {:segment seg, :pc 0}\\n            :stack []\\n            :k nil\\n            :halted? false\\n            :blocked? false\\n            :value nil))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; SemanticVM Protocol Implementation\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- vm-reset\\n  \\\"Reset execution state, preserving loaded code.\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790280923723-architect-ucf-phase1-signoff.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:46:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '190,390p' src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc; sed -n '1,155p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\";; =============================================================================\\n;; The canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2, §7.5)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(def vector-operand-table\\n  \\\"§2.4's operand table in UCF §7.3.2's positional form: mnemonic → ordered\\n   `[attribute kind]` operands. Position `i` of a canonical tuple (from 1)\\n   holds the operand entry `i-1` names; this one table drives the §7.5\\n   validator's rules. Each kind names the\\n   rule that judges it: `:data`, `:sym`, `:syms`, `:kw`, `:str`, `:bool`,\\n   and `:buffer` belong to `:operand-kind` (a nil passes it and is\\n   `:saturation`'s defect); `:pc` to `:target-bounds`; `:uint` to `:argc`.\\\"\\n  {:const [[:yin.code/value :data]],\\n   :var [[:yin.code/name :sym]],\\n   :closure [[:yin.code/params :syms] [:yin.code/body :pc]],\\n   :push [],\\n   :call [[:yin.code/argc :uint] [:yin.code/tail? :bool]],\\n   :return [],\\n   :jump [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\\n   :branch-false [[:yin.code/target :pc]],\\n   :halt [],\\n   :gensym [[:yin.code/prefix :str]],\\n   :store-get [[:yin.code/key :data]],\\n   :store-put [[:yin.code/key :data] [:yin.code/value :data]],\\n   :stream-make [[:yin.code/buffer :buffer]],\\n   :stream-put [],\\n   :stream-cursor [],\\n   :stream-next [],\\n   :stream-close [],\\n   :park [],\\n   :resume [[:yin.code/parked-id :kw]],\\n   :current-continuation [],\\n   :ffi-call [[:yin.code/ffi-op :kw] [:yin.code/argc :uint]]})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private operand-kind-checks\\n  \\\"§7.5 :operand-kind — kind → predicate. Absent kinds (`:pc`, `:uint`) are\\n   not judged by the rule: `:target-bounds` and `:argc` own them. A nil in\\n   a saturated slot passes here; `:saturation` owns it, the §7.4 `:slot-kind`\\n   precedent.\\\"\\n  {:data vm/plain-data?,\\n   :sym symbol?,\\n   :syms (fn [x] (and (vector? x) (every? symbol? x))),\\n   :kw keyword?,\\n   :str (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (string? x))),\\n   :bool (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (boolean? x))),\\n   :buffer (fn [x] (or (nil? x) (count? x)))})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private saturated-operands\\n  \\\"§7.5 :saturation — the `[mnemonic index]` operands a canonical tuple\\n   materializes (UCF §7.3.2); a nil in one of them is that rule's defect.\\\"\\n  #{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]})\\n\\n\\n(defn- tuple-defect\\n  [rule pc]\\n  {:rule rule, :pc pc})\\n\\n\\n(defn- mnemonic-of\\n  \\\"The mnemonic of one tuple, or nil when the element has no first element\\n   that could be one. A tuple is a vector — UCF §7.3.2's positional form —\\n   so a list element has no mnemonic.\\\"\\n  [t]\\n  (when (and (vector? t) (seq t))\\n    (nth t 0)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- tuple-kinds\\n  \\\"The operand kinds of one tuple's mnemonic, in tuple order.\\\"\\n  [t]\\n  (mapv second (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\\n\\n\\n;; Each rule below takes the whole vector and returns a defect or nil. Rules\\n;; run in §7.5's order and the first defect wins, so a rule may assume every\\n;; earlier one held — exactly as `rules` above runs over a batch.\\n\\n(defn- nonempty\\n  \\\"Rule 1. An empty vector names pc 0, where the first instruction belongs;\\n   so does anything a pc cannot index: the canonical form is a vector\\n   (UCF §7.3.2), so a list outer is not the form at any length.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (when-not (and (vector? v) (pos? (count v)))\\n    (tuple-defect :nonempty 0)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- mnemonic\\n  \\\"Rule 2.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some (fn [pc]\\n          (when-not (contains? mnemonics (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\\n            (tuple-defect :mnemonic pc)))\\n        (range (count v))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- arity\\n  \\\"Rule 3. §2.4's operand table as saturated and made positional by UCF\\n   §7.3.2: the mnemonic plus exactly its operands.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some (fn [pc]\\n          (let [t (nth v pc)]\\n            (when-not (= (inc (count (get vector-operand-table (nth t 0))))\\n                         (count t))\\n              (tuple-defect :arity pc))))\\n        (range (count v))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- operand-kind\\n  \\\"Rule 4. An operand is judged only by the kind §7.5 gives it.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some (fn [pc]\\n          (let [t (nth v pc)\\n                kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\\n            (some (fn [i]\\n                    (let [check (get operand-kind-checks (nth kinds (dec i)))]\\n                      (when (and check (not (check (nth t i))))\\n                        (tuple-defect :operand-kind pc))))\\n                  (range 1 (count t)))))\\n        (range (count v))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- saturation\\n  \\\"Rule 5. A saturated operand is materialized in a canonical tuple; a nil\\n   one names its pc.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some (fn [pc]\\n          (let [t (nth v pc)]\\n            (when (some #(and (contains? saturated-operands [(nth t 0) %])\\n                              (nil? (nth t %)))\\n                        (range 1 (count t)))\\n              (tuple-defect :saturation pc))))\\n        (range (count v))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- target-bounds\\n  \\\"Rule 6. A resolved pc must be an integer inside the segment.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (let [length (count v)]\\n    (some (fn [pc]\\n            (let [t (nth v pc)\\n                  kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\\n              (some (fn [i]\\n                      (when (= :pc (nth kinds (dec i)))\\n                        (let [x (nth t i)]\\n                          (when-not (and (integer? x) (<= 0 x (dec length)))\\n                            (tuple-defect :target-bounds pc)))))\\n                    (range 1 (count t)))))\\n          (range (count v)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- terminator\\n  \\\"Rule 7. Nothing labelled follows pc length-1, so the one instruction\\n   that can violate the rule is the last.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (let [pc (dec (count v))]\\n    (when-not (contains? terminators (mnemonic-of (nth v pc)))\\n      (tuple-defect :terminator pc))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- argc\\n  \\\"Rule 8. The :uint operands of `:call` and `:ffi-call`.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some (fn [pc]\\n          (let [t (nth v pc)\\n                kinds (tuple-kinds t)]\\n            (some (fn [i]\\n                    (when (= :uint (nth kinds (dec i)))\\n                      (when-not (count? (nth t i))\\n                        (tuple-defect :argc pc))))\\n                  (range 1 (count t)))))\\n        (range (count v))))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private vector-rules\\n  [nonempty mnemonic arity operand-kind saturation target-bounds terminator\\n   argc])\\n\\n\\n(defn well-formed-vector?\\n  \\\"Check one canonical instruction vector (UCF §7.3.2) against §7.5\\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`).\\n\\n   Returns nil when the vector is well formed, else the first defect as\\n   `{:rule r :pc p}` — `:nonempty`, `:mnemonic`, `:arity`,\\n   `:operand-kind`, `:saturation`, `:target-bounds`, `:terminator`, or\\n   `:argc` — with p the offending pc (`:nonempty` names pc 0, where the\\n   missing first instruction belongs). Rules run in §7.5's order, each\\n   assuming the earlier ones held. §2.6 rules 1–4 are satisfied by the\\n   vector form by construction (pc is the index); rules 5–6 and the UCF\\n   §7.3.4 checks are the eight here. The address check — a vector hashing\\n   to the address it claims — is not a structural rule and is not made\\n   here: a correct hash is never structural validation.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (some #(% v) vector-rules))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Segment rows (§6.2)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n(ns dao.jing\\n  \\\"DaoJing: the content-addressed storage observer (docs/design/dao.jing.md).\\n\\n   A content-store handle is plain data, not a protocol:\\n   {:put-content-fn f, :get-content-fn g, :close-fn c}. The backend effects\\n   are explicit functions: materialize! computes the content address solely\\n   from the payload, inserts idempotently, and returns the address only\\n   after the backend reports durability. get reads only registered\\n   :segment/<algorithm>-... content addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable roots\\n   are outside DaoJing.\\n\\n   The observer (observer-state / observe-step! / adopt-cursor) coordinates\\n   an explicit intake pool of dao.stream reader handles and materializes\\n   every payload through dao.stream.observe/step. Pool membership and\\n   every member's initial cursor are supplied by the caller; statuses and\\n   the scheduling index are ordinary immutable data. There are no atoms,\\n   globals, registration, or discovery, and the source stream never enters\\n   an address or a stored value.\\\"\\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [get])\\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\\n            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]\\n            #?@(:cljs [[goog.crypt :as crypt]\\n                       goog.crypt.Sha256\\n                       [\\\"@noble/hashes/blake3.js\\\" :as noble-blake3]\\n                       [\\\"@noble/hashes/utils.js\\\" :as noble-utils]])\\n            #?@(:cljd [[\\\"dart:convert\\\" :as convert]\\n                       [\\\"package:blake3_dart/blake3_dart.dart\\\"\\n                        :as blake3-dart]]))\\n  #?@(:cljd [(:import [\\\"dart:typed_data\\\" Uint8List])]))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private content-missing\\n  \\\"Internal not-found sentinel used to verify :present read-backs. An opaque\\n   per-host identity object, never a keyword: :dao.jing/content-missing is a\\n   legal opaque payload, and a keyword sentinel would be ambiguous with a\\n   genuinely stored value. Never exposed: a conforming backend cannot store\\n   or return it.\\\"\\n  #?(:cljd (Object.)\\n     :clj (Object.)\\n     :cljs (js-obj)))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Content addressing (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical encoding)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- canonical-print\\n  \\\"Print an order-normalized value following Clojure's printing conventions\\n   byte for byte — space-separated sequential elements, `, `-separated map\\n   entries, metadata as a ^m prefix — instead of delegating to the host\\n   printer, which drops collection metadata in at least one case (a sorted\\n   set prints through its metadata-less seq on Dart). Scalars still print\\n   through pr-str.\\\"\\n  [n]\\n  (let [prefixed (fn [body]\\n                   (if (meta n)\\n                     (str \\\"^\\\" (canonical-print (meta n)) \\\" \\\" body)\\n                     body))]\\n    (cond (map? n)\\n          (prefixed\\n            (str \\\"{\\\"\\n                 (str/join \\\", \\\" (map (fn [[k v]]\\n                                       (str (canonical-print k) \\\" \\\"\\n                                            (canonical-print v)))\\n                                     n))\\n                 \\\"}\\\"))\\n          (set? n)\\n          (prefixed\\n            (str \\\"#{\\\" (str/join \\\" \\\" (map canonical-print n)) \\\"}\\\"))\\n          (vector? n)\\n          (prefixed\\n            (str \\\"[\\\" (str/join \\\" \\\" (map canonical-print n)) \\\"]\\\"))\\n          (sequential? n)\\n          (prefixed\\n            (str \\\"(\\\" (str/join \\\" \\\" (map canonical-print n)) \\\")\\\"))\\n          :else (pr-str n))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- order-normalize\\n  \\\"Normalize a value so equal values print identically: maps and sets sort by\\n   canonically-printed key/element, sequences recurse, and collection metadata is\\n   normalized and reattached so it survives into the address. Reader position\\n   metadata (:line/:column and friends) is not content and is dropped; every\\n   other metadata difference changes the address.\\n\\n   A normalized set stays a (sorted) set and therefore prints with #{}\\n   braces: the set-ness marker comes from the printer, never from a tag like\\n   '(set ...) placed inside the ordinary value domain where a real list of\\n   that shape could collide with it.\\n\\n   Records are not a supported payload: the hosts cannot even agree on how\\n   to print one (tagged literal on the JVM and JS, plain map on Dart), so a\\n   record and its equal plain map would collide somewhere. order-normalize\\n   throws on records — anywhere in the value — rather than silently\\n   addressing them as maps.\\n\\n   Transitional: this exists only to make the print-based content hash\\n   deterministic and order-insensitive until the pinned, cross-platform\\n   canonical byte encoding lands (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical\\n   encoding). It is NOT that canonical encoding.\\\"\\n  [v]\\n  (let [attach-meta (fn [normalized]\\n                      (let [;; reader position is not content: the hosts'\\n                            ;; readers stamp source coordinates onto list\\n                            ;; literals, which would make an address depend\\n                            ;; on where a payload was written. Everything\\n                            ;; else in the metadata is address-significant.\\n                            m (dissoc (meta v)\\n                                      :line :column :end-line :end-column)]\\n                        ;; empty metadata is dropped: = ignores metadata\\n                        ;; entirely, and the hosts disagree on whether ^{}\\n                        ;; prints (the JVM skips it, Dart prints it)\\n                        (if (seq m)\\n                          (with-meta normalized (order-normalize m))\\n                          normalized)))]\\n    (cond (record? v)\\n          (throw (ex-info \\\"dao.jing does not address records: hosts print them differently, so their addresses would collide across hosts\\\"\\n                          {:payload v}))\\n          (map? v) (attach-meta\\n                     (->> v\\n                          (map (fn [[k x]]\\n                                 [(order-normalize k)\\n                                  (order-normalize x)]))\\n                          ;; a canonically-printed-keyed sorted map prints\\n                          ;; its keys in a fixed order on every platform\\n                          ;; (array-map is not in ClojureDart)\\n                          (into (sorted-map-by\\n                                  #(compare (canonical-print %1)\\n                                            (canonical-print %2))))))\\n          (set? v) (attach-meta\\n                     ;; a sorted set prints its elements in one fixed order\\n                     ;; on every platform and keeps its #{} braces, so it can\\n                     ;; never print like the list or vector of the same\\n                     ;; elements\\n                     (into (sorted-set-by\\n                             #(compare (canonical-print %1)\\n                                       (canonical-print %2)))\\n                           (map order-normalize v)))\\n          (vector? v) (attach-meta (mapv order-normalize v))\\n          ;; lists and seqs are one canonical value: = calls them equal and\\n          ;; both print (e1 e2 ...), so both normalize to a list. The\\n          ;; with-meta nil is load-bearing: ClojureDart's list returns a list\\n          ;; carrying cljd.core's own reader metadata (:line, :tag\\n          ;; PersistentList, ...), which would otherwise print into the address\\n          (sequential? v) (attach-meta\\n                            (with-meta (apply list (map order-normalize v))\\n                              nil))\\n          :else v)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- order-normalized-print\\n  \\\"Transitional encoder: canonical-print over the order-normalized form,\\n   which prints collection metadata instead of dropping it. NOT the final\\n   canonical byte encoding; see order-normalize.\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"The test harness calls `repl/create-state` and `repl/eval-input`, and its direct media checks use `dao.stream/cursor` and `next` with a separate cursor. That fits the intended boundary. I’m checking the host evidence and whether any assertions accidentally encode a shell implementation detail as an architectural rule."}}
