n brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an\n222:Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization\n223:boundary. An in-memory stream whose admission declaration permits host\n241:somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n253:(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler\n307:identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —\n338:acquisition completes is not creating a stream and deferring its medium; it is\n339:deferring the creation itself, and `ok` would be false. Such a transport does\n359:{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!\n360:                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}\n379:ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor\n390:honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n400:answer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n404:answering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n405:trails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n408:declaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n422:one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n429:its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n431:public surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n449:reports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n450:evicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n459:a callback, on a thread or event loop DaoStream does not own. DaoStream does\n463:every reader above observes it with its own cursor, in its own control flow,\n475:A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n477:- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n478:  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n485:  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n486:  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n487:  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n490:  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n493:  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n494:  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n495:  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n497:  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n503:  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n505:  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n507:  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n509:  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n512:  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n515:  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n517:- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n519:  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n520:  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n521:- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n522:  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n523:  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n526:- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n527:  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n528:  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n529:- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n531:  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n532:- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n533:  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n534:  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n538:`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n544:| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |\n546:| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |\n547:| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |\n548:| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |\n549:| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
