{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc | sed -n '72,150p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc | sed -n '160,250p'\"","aggregated_output":"    72\t\n    73\t;; =============================================================================\n    74\t;; Effect descriptors\n    75\t;; =============================================================================\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn effect?\n    78\t  \"Check if a value is an effect descriptor.\"\n    79\t  [x]\n    80\t  (and (map? x) (contains? x :effect)))\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\t(defn make-effect\n    84\t  \"Create an effect descriptor.\"\n    85\t  [effect-type & {:as params}]\n    86\t  (assoc params :effect effect-type))\n    87\t\n    88\t\n    89\t;; =============================================================================\n    90\t;; The `stream` module\n    91\t;; =============================================================================\n    92\t;;\n    93\t;; These are pure data constructors. They touch no stream: Yin source calls\n    94\t;; them through the module system, they return effect maps, and the engine\n    95\t;; interprets those. `take!` is deliberately absent — destructive read is one\n    96\t;; reader's progress and every other reader's data loss, and a v2 `take!` would\n    97\t;; need the reader-position-in-the-medium the contract retired.\n    98\t\n    99\t(defn make\n   100\t  ([] (make nil))\n   101\t  ([cap] {:effect :stream/make, :capacity cap}))\n   102\t\n   103\t\n   104\t(defn put!\n   105\t  [s v]\n   106\t  {:effect :stream/put, :stream s, :val v})\n   107\t\n   108\t\n   109\t(defn cursor\n   110\t  [s]\n   111\t  {:effect :stream/cursor, :stream s})\n   112\t\n   113\t\n   114\t(defn next!\n   115\t  [c]\n   116\t  {:effect :stream/next, :cursor c})\n   117\t\n   118\t\n   119\t(defn close!\n   120\t  [s]\n   121\t  {:effect :stream/close, :stream s})\n   122\t\n   123\t\n   124\t(def stream-module\n   125\t  \"The v2 `stream` module definition. Registering it is a composition step.\"\n   126\t  {'make make, 'put! put!, 'cursor cursor, 'next! next!, 'close! close!})\n   127\t\n   128\t\n   129\t(defn register-stream-module\n   130\t  \"Return registry with the v2 `stream` module registered.\n   131\t   A composition that calls this must also supply `:make-stream`, or\n   132\t   `(stream/make)` is unsupported and says so.\"\n   133\t  [registry]\n   134\t  (register-module registry 'stream stream-module))\n   135\t\n   136\t\n   137\t;; =============================================================================\n   138\t;; Built-in effect handlers\n   139\t;; =============================================================================\n   140\t\n   141\t(defn require-handler\n   142\t  \"Resolve `:module/require` against the registry value only.\n   143\t\n   144\t   v1's clj branch called `clojure.core/require` from inside effect dispatch —\n   145\t   a host call in the middle of interpretation. It does not survive: a module\n   146\t   is either in the registry the composition supplied or it is not.\"\n   147\t  [state effect _opts]\n   148\t  (let [module-name (:module effect)]\n   149\t    (if (some? (resolve-module (:modules state) module-name))\n   150\t      {:value module-name, :state state, :blocked? false}\n   160\t\n   161\t\n   162\t(defn handle-make\n   163\t  \"Handle :stream/make. Creates a stream through the composition's\n   164\t   `:make-stream` and stores the handle.\n   165\t\n   166\t   A nil capacity has no v2 meaning, so the module path defaults exactly as\n   167\t   the AST path does. Absent a supplied constructor this fails and says so;\n   168\t   silently falling back to a private transport is the failure mode the rule\n   169\t   exists to prevent.\n   170\t   Returns [stream-ref updated-state].\"\n   171\t  [state effect id]\n   172\t  (let [capacity (or (:capacity effect) vm/default-stream-capacity)\n   173\t        handle (vm/create-stream! (:make-stream state) capacity :stream/make)\n   174\t        new-store (assoc (:store state) id handle)\n   175\t        stream-ref {:type :stream-ref, :id id}]\n   176\t    [stream-ref (assoc state :store new-store)]))\n   177\t\n   178\t\n   179\t(defn handle-put\n   180\t  \"Handle :stream/put. Total over the five append outcomes.\n   181\t   Returns {:value v :state s} on success, {:park true :stream-id id :state s}\n   182\t   on `full`. `closed`, `invalid-value` and `transport-error` are errors that\n   183\t   name their outcome, as v1's throw on a closed stream did.\"\n   184\t  [state effect]\n   185\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   186\t        val (:val effect)\n   187\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   188\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   189\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   190\t    (let [result (stream/append! handle val)\n   191\t          o (outcome result)]\n   192\t      (case o\n   193\t        :dao.stream/ok {:value val, :state state}\n   194\t        :dao.stream/full {:park true, :stream-id stream-id, :state state}\n   195\t        (fail \"Stream append failed\" {:outcome o, :stream-id stream-id})))))\n   196\t\n   197\t\n   198\t(defn handle-cursor\n   199\t  \"Handle :stream/cursor. Mints an opaque cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`.\n   200\t\n   201\t   v1 fabricated `{:position 0}` and touched no stream. Minting is a stream\n   202\t   operation, so `closed` and `transport-error` arrive here.\n   203\t   Returns [cursor-ref updated-state].\"\n   204\t  [state effect id]\n   205\t  (let [stream-ref (:stream effect)\n   206\t        stream-id (:id stream-ref)\n   207\t        handle (get (:store state) stream-id)]\n   208\t    (when (nil? handle) (fail \"Invalid stream reference\" {:ref stream-ref}))\n   209\t    (let [cursor (vm/mint-oldest handle :stream/cursor)\n   210\t          cursor-data (vm/cursor-entry stream-id cursor)\n   211\t          new-store (assoc (:store state) id cursor-data)\n   212\t          cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id id}]\n   213\t      [cursor-ref (assoc state :store new-store)])))\n   214\t\n   215\t\n   216\t(defn handle-next\n   217\t  \"Handle :stream/next. Total over the seven read outcomes.\n   218\t\n   219\t   `ok` advances the store cursor to the exact returned successor. `blocked`\n   220\t   parks. `end` yields nil, as v1 did. `gap` advances to the recovery cursor\n   221\t   and yields `:dao.stream/gap`, so a program that reads it learns values were\n   222\t   lost. The three terminal outcomes are errors.\"\n   223\t  [state effect]\n   224\t  (let [cursor-ref (:cursor effect)\n   225\t        cursor-id (:id cursor-ref)\n   226\t        store (:store state)\n   227\t        cursor-data (get store cursor-id)]\n   228\t    (when (nil? cursor-data) (fail \"Invalid cursor reference\" {:ref cursor-ref}))\n   229\t    (let [stream-id (:stream-id cursor-data)\n   230\t          handle (get store stream-id)]\n   231\t      (when (nil? handle)\n   232\t        (fail \"Stream not found for cursor\" {:stream-id stream-id}))\n   233\t      (let [result (stream/next handle (:cursor cursor-data))\n   234\t            o (outcome result)\n   235\t            advance (fn [state* value]\n   236\t                      {:value value,\n   237\t                       :state (assoc state*\n   238\t                                     :store\n   239\t                                     (assoc store\n   240\t                                            cursor-id\n   241\t                                            (assoc cursor-data\n   242\t                                                   :cursor\n   243\t                                                   (:dao.stream/cursor\n   244\t                                                     result))))})]\n   245\t        (case o\n   246\t          :dao.stream/ok (advance state (:dao.stream/value result))\n   247\t          :dao.stream/blocked {:park true,\n   248\t                               :cursor-ref cursor-ref,\n   249\t                               :stream-id stream-id,\n   250\t                               :state state}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
